WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:15.040
PRESENTER: Thank you very much.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.670
It's a pleasure and
an honor to be here.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:23.040
And we all look forward
very much to the discussion

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.260
that we are about to have.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:32.610
Our speaker today is
Mr. Roger Boisjoly.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.860
He tells me that I
should introduce him

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.330
by saying that for 27
years, he has been active

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:43.290
in mechanical design and
structural engineering

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.240
in the aerospace industry.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.920
He has worked for
some 14 companies,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.030
of which at least one you
must be familiar with.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:58.470
And he did not mention
this, but I will.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.590
In 1988, the
American Association

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:05.430
for the Advancement
of Science awarded him

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:14.800
the prize for ethical behavior
as a result of his activities

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.930
which followed the Challenger
accident that most of you

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:21.910
are familiar with.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:25.630
He will talk to us about
engineering ethics,

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:29.680
constructive responses
to difficult situations.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.090
And we have asked a number
of friends and colleagues

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:40.670
to comment on his remarks
and to give their--

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:43.460
testify to their
experience, bear witness

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.930
with their experiences,
in issues of the sort.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.090
We have three panelists.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.780
Miss Freada Klein
who is the president

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.090
of Klein associates,
a consulting

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.640
firm which deals with--

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:10.110
which advises corporations on
the ways in which one adjusts,

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:15.300
in an ethical and proper way,
relationships between employees

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.800
and employers.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.170
Professor Melcher
who is professor

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:28.610
of electrical engineering
and computer science.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:36.710
And he has been active in
electrostatics and a number

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.350
of other interesting fields.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:46.260
He has also been active in
worrying about the role--

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.440
the connection between
the academic community

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.340
and the Strategic
Defense Initiative,

00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:00.320
and thinking about this
has brought home to him

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.370
a number of questions that
we all need to worry about.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.640
And Professor David Marks, who
is the professor of and head

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:17.790
of the civil engineering
department, and his region--

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:23.670
his area of activity is
environmental engineering

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:30.430
and management and control,
primarily the public sector

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:34.780
thereof, which raises,
again, a substantial number

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.930
of interesting
ethical questions.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:43.310
I think the way to proceed is
going to be for Mr. Boisjoly

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.320
to give us his remarks.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:51.400
We will then let the
panelists make their comments

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:56.610
and then have a
general discussion.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:00.150
Mr. Boisjoly, we are
honored to hear from you.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.760
BOISJOLY: Thank you very much.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.040
This talk is based
on my 27 years

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.700
experience in the aerospace
industry at 14 companies.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.310
I'm going to recommend to you,
some of the methods I used

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:24.280
to secure a productive
and satisfying career.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:28.190
I will suggest how to select
your entry-level employer,

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:32.430
explain the task of getting
settled in a new position,

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.640
and how to recognize and
deal with real organizational

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.230
trouble.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.990
I will give you some examples
of my career confrontations

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.950
with lessons learned and
summarize with a few challenges

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.240
to you.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.430
How to select your
entry-level employer

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:51.790
is what I'll deal with first.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:53.290
Seek employment
with a company that

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:56.650
has a large commercial
mix of business

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.590
rather than a government
dependent military or aerospace

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:00.919
base.

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:02.210
There are two reasons for this.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.960
First, a mixed
commercial business,

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.120
even if combined with
some government business,

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.340
will provide a
better opportunity

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:10.450
for long-term stability.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:13.840
And second, the company
is more likely to be

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.480
responsive to conduct
business on a higher

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.320
ethical plane due to the
inherent commercial marketplace

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.180
accountability that exists.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:24.830
Commercial marketplace
accountability

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:28.730
has the effect of
placing management

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:31.860
at business risk for
future of their company

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.730
as opposed to a government
contractor company that

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:38.480
essentially has zero business
risk or accountability,

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:41.420
even if caught conducting
unethical or fraudulent

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.517
business practices.

00:05:43.517 --> 00:05:45.100
The evidence to
support this condition

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:48.310
abounds in the media with
many examples of wrongdoing

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:50.770
by many of the
government contractors,

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:55.120
without any of them ever losing
their contractor certification

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.460
to bid on those contracts.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.640
Their fines after
being caught are

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.060
almost laughable if it
weren't for the seriousness

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.000
of the situation.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.850
Since they are delivering
faulty hardware

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.970
that doesn't come close
to meeting specifications

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:15.690
and are having huge profits
without accountability.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:19.810
An ideal company has
approximately 1,000 employees

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:23.350
reporting to one general manager
at one physical location.

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:25.530
This number is not
cast in concrete

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.990
but is a good starting point.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.780
This size limitation
is very important,

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:34.140
since it determines how well
a company or division can

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:37.950
be managed as well as
its operating philosophy.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:41.620
Read the book titled
In Search of Excellence

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:45.040
for a detailed rationale of
why this size limitation is

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:46.490
important.

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:49.220
My 27 years experience
mirrors the position

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.440
stated in that
book, and I heartily

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:55.040
subscribe to the small
size, family type atmosphere

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.420
organization that provides
the best career opportunity

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.680
and environment over all others.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.510
Interviews, especially
at the entry level,

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:09.220
seem totally one-sided, with the
company doing its best to sell

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:11.560
itself, and their organization--

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:14.530
that their organization
offers the best opportunity

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:16.180
for a professional career.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.520
They're one-sided simply
because students generally

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:20.800
allow it to become
one-sided for fear

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.710
of not obtaining a job and
a good quality interview.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:28.940
And this is quite normal,
but you have an obligation

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:31.640
to yourself to ascertain
more about the company

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:33.446
than they will just tell you.

00:07:33.446 --> 00:07:35.570
You must attempt to find
out about their management

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.060
philosophy and
organizational structure,

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:42.560
so you can evaluate if they
match your minimum ideals.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:46.250
For example, ask to
whom the quality control

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:47.870
manager reports.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:50.360
Also, ask if they have
an ombudsman program

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:53.460
and to whom that
ombudsman reports.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:54.960
The answers to
these two questions

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.080
alone will provide great
insight to their management

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.580
and operating philosophy.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.560
It will not guarantee
anything, but it will

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.900
provide significant insight.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.200
For example, if neither
the quality control manager

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.070
or the ombudsman reports to the
top executive at that facility,

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.800
then this is a red
flag of caution

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.780
because this may be a
deliberate management

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.580
flaw in the
organization to promote

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.580
the ability of other
line organizations

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:22.760
to extract cooperation
with their agenda

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.410
by intimidation of
the quality control

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:28.370
manager or the ombudsman.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.350
And I can tell you
as a sidelight,

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:33.200
if the ombudsman reports to the
personnel department, that's

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:35.679
two red flags of caution.

00:08:35.679 --> 00:08:38.049
I assure you that this type
of management organization

00:08:38.049 --> 00:08:42.130
creates the ideal climate
for unethical misuse of line

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:45.400
organization power to meet the
goals of short-term profits

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:48.730
and delivery schedules, and
keep top management segregated

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:50.800
from the real
day-to-day operations,

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:53.840
but very happy with
the results provided.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.280
Another important
question to ask

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.420
is, does a top executive hold
at least a yearly meeting

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:02.600
to give a sort of state of
the company status review

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:04.820
to its employees.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:07.670
Well-managed companies
generally tend towards this type

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:09.170
of communication from the top.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:11.910
It is a very motivating time.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.120
And the results are
usually recognized

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.422
by these companies that do it.

00:09:16.422 --> 00:09:18.130
I believe that the
most important request

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.080
you can make during
any interview

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:23.200
is to ask to speak in
private to your future peer

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:26.050
group in the departments
which you are being considered

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:29.080
for employment and ask
the following questions

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:31.220
as a minimum.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:35.330
What do you like or
dislike about this company?

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:38.930
Would you recommend coming
to work here at this time?

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:40.760
Do you like working
in this department?

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.580
Or would you prefer working
in another department?

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:46.790
Is your supervisor and manager a
strong, but supportive and fair

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:48.630
individual?

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:50.820
Are you required to
work excessive overtime

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:54.090
with or without compensation
at the whim of supervision,

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:56.850
whether it is needed or not?

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:00.740
You will be amazed at the candid
answers that you will receive.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.900
But be sure to talk to
more than one person

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.090
to minimize the
possibility of talking only

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:07.550
to a disgruntled employee.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:09.920
Also, be prepared to answer
some questions about why

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.570
you are asking such questions.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:15.060
I hope you have concluded by
now, that all this requires

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:18.120
some detailed preparation
so you can present yourself

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.540
in a smooth, relaxed,
and sincere manner.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:23.250
Practice with your
fellow job-seeking

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:27.450
peers and always remember that
companies are seeking the best

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.820
employees for their
organizations,

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:32.130
and you have your
talents to offer

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:35.160
in exchange for a good ethical
work environment and career

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.720
opportunity.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.990
I sincerely believe that many
companies have come full circle

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:43.329
and realize that their employees
are their number one asset.

00:10:43.329 --> 00:10:45.120
And they've completed
or are in the process

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:46.560
of shedding their
form of business

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:51.120
as usual mode of operation in
favor of organizational changes

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.600
which will provide open,
objective discussion

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.290
of problems.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:11:04.010
Now, that you've had a chance
to get a entry-level position.

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:06.010
Let's talk about
the task of getting

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:09.560
settled in that profession
that you've selected.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.240
Companies generally
use two methods

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.500
to introduce you to
their organization.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:16.130
One is to assign
you to read what

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:18.950
seems to be a never-ending
stack of policies and procedures

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:20.480
manuals.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:23.750
And the other is to give you a
short synopsis of the contents

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:26.660
of those manuals and assign
an informal mentor who

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:29.330
will help you get acquainted
with their procedures.

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:32.290
If you are unlucky enough
to get the form a method,

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:34.670
then a word of advice
is appropriate,

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:37.760
don't waste your time
reading in detail

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:39.800
because the day-to-day
operations of almost

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:41.750
every company has no
resemblance to what's

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:43.740
written in the manuals.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:47.670
Be an attentive observer of
who and how things get done,

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:50.430
and take every opportunity
to attend meetings,

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:53.880
and ask to do so if
not invited to do so.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:57.690
You can assess-- so you can
assess the players quickly.

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:00.120
At first, be happy with
your role as observer,

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.700
and be attentive to
who is participating

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:04.200
in the discussions.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.200
Identify the managers and who
is controlling the meeting,

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:08.700
and if the discussions
appear to be

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:13.170
objective with factual depth,
or just bland rhetoric.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:15.870
It is very important to
assess as quickly as possible,

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:18.120
if meeting content is
almost totally controlled

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:20.520
by management, or if
technical subordinates

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:22.620
are allowed to engage
in wide-open discussion

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:24.290
of problems.

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:27.000
One surefire way to
make this assessment

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.950
is to observe the
person with the highest

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:30.990
authority at any meeting.

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.360
If that person is usually
quiet until near the end

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:37.260
of the meeting, then it means he
is truly interested in problem

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:38.490
resolution.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:40.320
But if that person
usually speaks first

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:42.510
about technical
content, then he is

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:45.330
using the meeting for
his own personal agenda,

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:48.030
and little meaningful
problem resolution discussion

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:49.760
will ensue.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:53.420
Spend as much time as possible
outside your assigned area

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:55.640
talking with people
from other departments,

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:58.610
and soliciting their
help, and offering yours.

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:00.890
Be sincere about
this because it will

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:03.950
become a very important
asset for you in the future.

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:08.000
I use this very method to
develop name, competency,

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:11.330
and productivity recognition
by others, especially those

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:12.480
in management positions.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.070
And this was a key element
for me in any organization,

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:18.590
with the sole exception of
being in the right place,

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:21.140
at the right time,
with the right mentor,

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:23.680
and that never happened to me.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:25.210
Don't ever be
afraid to speak out

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:26.830
in support of a
technical position

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:28.450
you have confidence with.

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:31.450
But also don't be afraid to
say, I don't know the answer

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:34.000
to that question,
instead of trying

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:38.050
to bluff an answer to, and lose
your precious integrity, which

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:41.626
will result in long-term
damages to your career.

00:13:41.626 --> 00:13:43.500
It will take approximately
six to nine months

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:46.320
to feel comfortable
within the organization.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.810
And then it is time to start
the serious documentation

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:51.090
of your work product
by writing memos, which

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:54.000
are carefully prepared to
catch the eye of your peers

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.630
and management.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:59.790
All memos must contain
three basic features.

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:03.030
First, the subject of the memo
must be clear and concise.

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:05.120
It sounds very simple,
but oftentimes, this

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:08.810
is one of the most
brutalized aspect of a memo.

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:12.140
Second, the memo must contain
a complete executive summary

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:15.800
stating the problem,
solution, recommendations,

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:19.190
and a conclusion in a
maximum of one page.

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:22.790
I don't mean executive summary
plus introduction spills over

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:23.630
into second page.

00:14:23.630 --> 00:14:27.590
One physical page is the maximum
that an executive summary

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:28.740
should be contained in.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:30.790
It should never spill
to the second page ever.

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:35.870
Third, the detailed discussion
containing a much expanded

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:38.870
version of the executive summary
with all the supporting data

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:41.600
deemed necessary to complete
the documentation of your work

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:42.950
output.

00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:44.660
This memo format
is very important

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:47.030
because it lets you send
it to upper management,

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:48.530
within and outside
your department,

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:51.110
with a high probability
that they will read

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:54.320
the short executive
summary, and the whole memo

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:57.410
if the executive summary
piques their interest.

00:14:57.410 --> 00:15:00.800
I highly recommend the method
stated thus far, but only,

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:03.560
only, and I emphasize
only, if you

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:06.620
are genuinely interested
in receiving recognition,

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:10.310
and are willing to participate
in an accelerated and increased

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:14.010
portion of productivity output.

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:15.470
Now, that we're
settled, how do we

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:18.770
go about spotting real and
serious organizational trouble,

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:21.020
and what to do about it?

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:25.040
You will learn from experience
that very few projects run

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:29.155
smooth and
trouble-free, regardless

00:15:29.155 --> 00:15:30.530
of the amount of
talent assembled

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:33.440
to solve the problems,
because people always

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:37.460
have different levels of ego,
competence, self-esteem, et

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:38.840
cetera.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:43.460
I define real trouble as
a recognized deficiency

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:45.710
in a product, which has
a reasonable probability

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:50.190
of causing a serious
problem to the end-user,

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:52.170
but is not acknowledged
by management to be

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:54.760
a serious problem
that needs fixing.

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:56.560
The mode of operation
that usually precedes

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:59.920
this definition is one that
I have already mentioned,

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:02.050
when the person with the
highest level authority

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:05.260
controls the technical
meetings, and also exercises

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:07.960
absolute control on
how presentations

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:12.070
are prepared and presented, both
internally and to customers.

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:13.930
This type of
organizational misconduct

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:15.340
places the technical
professional

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:19.650
in a very difficult position
between the company management

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:22.170
and the customer, especially
when the technical people

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:23.730
are expected to
defend the management

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:27.060
position, whether
technically correct or not.

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:28.560
Another clue to
look for is the lack

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:31.440
of personal recognition
for exceptionally good work

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:35.430
and a high level of productivity
at merit review time.

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:37.410
This is usually a subtle,
but highly visible,

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:40.260
message being sent to you
by someone in management

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:43.870
that you do not fit their
definition of a team player.

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:46.890
If you receive two non-deserved
bad merit reviews in a row,

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:49.260
this is more than
a subtle suggestion

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:52.060
of your fall from favor
with the hierarchy.

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:55.560
This is one of the least
adversarial and most effective

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:59.850
management tools used to
crush a person's ethical fiber

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:01.980
and is used often as
a tool to encourage

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:04.440
people to leave voluntarily.

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:07.740
The most serious trouble of all
comes when meeting schedules

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:10.920
supersedes everything else,
especially the requirements

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:12.839
of safety and quality.

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:15.660
This is the toughest
category of serious trouble

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:19.589
to contest because shipments
produce revenue, which maintain

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:23.280
short-term profits, and keep
executive management very happy

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:24.510
indeed.

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:27.750
Be advised that there is
no way for an individual

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.240
to ever win or force
change of operation

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:32.940
in this category of
trouble without committing

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:37.230
sure and swift career
suicide, unless you

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.576
are able to gather colleague
support for your position.

00:17:40.576 --> 00:17:42.825
If technical support is
received from your colleagues,

00:17:42.825 --> 00:17:45.930
then attempt to obtain their
backing and cooperation,

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:48.540
and meet with management
to discuss the problem

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:50.250
and proposed solution.

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:51.990
You have a loyalty
to the company

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:55.200
you work for to
first seek problem

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.270
resolutions through your
organizational chain

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:58.020
of command.

00:17:58.020 --> 00:18:01.110
Don't ever go outside without
first giving them a chance

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:03.030
to correct the situation.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:05.300
If management is
not receptive, then

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:07.350
attempt to publish
a position paper

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:10.410
with the support of as many
colleagues as possible,

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:12.750
and send it up through the
management organization.

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:14.880
You may find that when
you put things on paper,

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.760
some of that support
will diminish.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:19.620
If management also
rejects this approach,

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:22.650
it is now becomes
personal decision time,

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:24.660
and three distinct
options exist,

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:26.500
which are most
effectively stated

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:30.750
in Albert Hirschman's
book titled Exit, Voice,

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:32.280
and Loyalty.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:34.800
At this point of confrontation,
the individual decisions

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:38.790
are tough because your career
and peace of mind are at stake.

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:41.670
I cannot tell you what to
do, but I can assure you

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:43.770
of an intense
struggle from within,

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:46.410
and difficulty with restless
sleep if you are a person

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:48.000
of conscience.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:49.410
I do have some
advice that should

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:52.230
be followed if you
decide to write memos,

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:55.860
like I did at
Morton-Thiokol in 1985.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:57.780
You must first gather
all the written evidence

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:01.365
possible in the form of
memos, reports, drawings, et

00:19:01.365 --> 00:19:03.360
cetera, to support
your position,

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.190
and transport them
to a safe place

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:08.190
away from the
company or your home,

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:09.730
prior to writing
your first memo.

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:15.470
The serious memo writing
can now commence,

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:18.470
and should inform management in
the clear and simple language

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:22.040
about your concerns, so
management cannot, excuse me,

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:25.890
claim a misunderstanding
at a later date.

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:27.500
I was very lucky
at Morton-Thiokol

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:30.050
that my files were not
purged by the company

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:31.670
before I had an
opportunity to give

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:34.100
my information to the
Presidential Commission

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:36.740
investigating the Space
Shuttle Challenger disaster.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:38.690
I've thought long and
hard about the methods

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:41.150
I used to expose the
truth about Challenger.

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:43.760
And I have concluded that I
would do everything the same,

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:47.720
except I would protect all the
documentation well in advance

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:51.250
to avoid a lack of
evidence at a later date.

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.860
Now, I'd like to share some
personal examples of my career

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.150
confrontations.

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:58.330
The following examples
are in chronological order

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:00.850
to demonstrate how the
magnitude of confrontations

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:03.310
increase in both severity
and frequency with years

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:04.435
of experience.

00:20:04.435 --> 00:20:07.690
In 1960, I was at my first
job at Hamilton Standard

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:09.850
in Broad Brook, Connecticut.

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:12.640
A project engineer was
reviewing a rather extensive set

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:15.490
of gear train actuate
calculations with me.

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:17.572
And I noticed an error
in an equation, right

00:20:17.572 --> 00:20:19.280
at the very beginning
of the calculation,

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:22.510
but said nothing,
and let him continue.

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:24.790
As time passed, I could
no longer contain myself,

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:26.230
and I blurted out
that the results

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:30.209
were wrong due to an error at
the beginning of the analysis.

00:20:30.209 --> 00:20:31.750
He was shocked and
quickly challenged

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:33.040
me to show him the error.

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:34.870
And he became angry with me.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:36.490
So I apologized to
him for the delay

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:38.680
in acknowledging that error.

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:40.690
He composed himself
quickly, since he was not

00:20:40.690 --> 00:20:42.760
angry for me for
finding the error,

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:46.630
but angry that I took so
long to inform him of it.

00:20:46.630 --> 00:20:49.354
We became somewhat friendly
after this encounter.

00:20:49.354 --> 00:20:50.770
The lesson I learned
from this was

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:53.710
to never delay bringing
up bad news to anyone

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:56.380
because the delay
causes more discord

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:59.230
and really throws an
immense amount of suspicion

00:20:59.230 --> 00:21:00.940
on your integrity.

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:05.410
In 1963, I was at my third job
at Autonetics in California.

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:07.360
I was hired to perform
stress analysis

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:09.490
with the title of
design engineer,

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:11.650
and subsequently attempted
to obtain a transfer

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:14.680
to the official structures
group to enhance my analysis

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:16.780
opportunities and capabilities.

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:21.350
But my supervisor denied
the request to transfer.

00:21:21.350 --> 00:21:23.960
Upon hearing of layoffs,
and witnessing those layoffs

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:27.050
taking place at the company
of approximately 40,000 people

00:21:27.050 --> 00:21:30.230
at that division at the time,
I immediately sought a position

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:32.180
at another company
because I feared

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:34.700
that my request for transfer
had damaged my relationship

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:36.200
with that supervisor.

00:21:36.200 --> 00:21:39.430
I accepted another position
and submitted my resignation,

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:41.290
and my supervisor was shocked.

00:21:41.290 --> 00:21:44.610
And he tried very, very
hard to convince me to stay,

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:47.660
but I told him I had already
agreed and gave my word,

00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:49.230
and I left.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:51.590
The lesson I learned was
to always participate

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:53.820
in open discussion
about your position,

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:55.560
especially if you
feel it's in jeopardy.

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:58.440
In the example I just gave,
before accepting other employ,

00:21:58.440 --> 00:21:59.840
I was very happy there.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:02.570
But I thought I didn't stand
a prayer of staying there

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:04.670
with those massive layoffs
that were coming on,

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:07.790
and just don't make the
mistakes that I've made.

00:22:07.790 --> 00:22:11.930
In 1965, I found myself at my
sixth job at Hamilton Standard

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:13.950
Aerospace division--
totally different division

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:15.670
and a different
physical location

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:17.750
although it was the
same corporation.

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:20.540
My position was design engineer
on the Apollo Lunar Lander

00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:22.430
environmental control system.

00:22:22.430 --> 00:22:24.650
And I refused to sign
off on a discrepant part

00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:28.340
as I was being pressured by
a project engineer to do so.

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:31.067
A quality control manager had
observed the confrontation.

00:22:31.067 --> 00:22:33.650
And he approached me afterwards
with support and the following

00:22:33.650 --> 00:22:34.790
advice.

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:37.490
He told me to ask myself
the following question

00:22:37.490 --> 00:22:39.950
when faced with a tough decision
of whether a product was

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:43.610
acceptable or not, would you
allow your wife or children

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:46.790
to use the product
without any reservations?

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:48.350
If I could not
answer that question

00:22:48.350 --> 00:22:51.110
with an unqualified
yes, he said,

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:54.800
I should not sign off on that
product for others to use.

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:57.020
I have always used this
as a surefire method

00:22:57.020 --> 00:22:59.930
to defuse an ethical
dilemma concerning

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:01.000
the safety of a product.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:02.960
And I've realized
that it is really

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:06.200
a direct application and simple
application of the golden rule.

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:08.885
Do unto others as you would
have them do unto you.

00:23:08.885 --> 00:23:10.820
The lesson learned
was to always practice

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:13.160
personal and professional
ethical behavior,

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:15.950
and you'll go far in
your career for doing so.

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:20.450
In the 1967 to '73 time
frame, I was at my eighth job

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:23.030
at a company called
Celesco Industry.

00:23:23.030 --> 00:23:26.032
It was formerly a West
Coast Defense division

00:23:26.032 --> 00:23:27.740
of Atlantic Research
Corporation that had

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:29.900
been spun off to Susquehanna.

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:32.690
As a senior stress engineer, I
was selected as the engineering

00:23:32.690 --> 00:23:35.900
representative to inspect the
assembly of several Athena

00:23:35.900 --> 00:23:38.540
missiles at the launchpad
to assure compliance

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:40.820
to structural
design requirements.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:44.525
During one of my inspections, I
found a payload cable retention

00:23:44.525 --> 00:23:47.720
bolt was loaded and bending
across its threads, which

00:23:47.720 --> 00:23:52.430
is a no-no, due to improperly
aligned mounting surfaces.

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:55.040
The failure of this bolt
would destroy the nose cone

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:57.802
and ruin all chances for
a successful research

00:23:57.802 --> 00:23:58.760
and development flight.

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:02.420
So I reported my concerns
to the project manager

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:04.040
with my recommendation
to replace

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:07.190
the hardware with properly
designed surfaces.

00:24:07.190 --> 00:24:10.430
He refused to take action for
fear of delaying the launch.

00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:13.850
So I told him that I would call
the Air Force colonel myself

00:24:13.850 --> 00:24:15.110
and report my findings.

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:18.200
And he knew I meant it,
so he placed the call.

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:20.450
The Air Force agreed with
me, and immediately obtained

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:22.280
a high priority
work authorization

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:23.735
at a local machine shop.

00:24:23.735 --> 00:24:25.610
And the work was completed
during the weekend

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:27.650
without delaying the launch.

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:29.480
The colonel thanked
me, and I eventually

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:31.700
received company support
because the customer was

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:34.544
pleased with my performance.

00:24:34.544 --> 00:24:36.710
Another incident on the
same program at a later date

00:24:36.710 --> 00:24:39.620
involved a design mistake
in a tongue and groove joint

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:45.160
on a heat shield, which
continually failed in testing.

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:47.050
A customer interface
meeting was convened

00:24:47.050 --> 00:24:50.449
to discuss the problem, and
as the discussion continued

00:24:50.449 --> 00:24:52.990
for several hours, I discovered
what the cause of the problem

00:24:52.990 --> 00:24:56.890
was while tinkering with
several small parts on my lap.

00:24:56.890 --> 00:24:58.300
I was confident
with my findings,

00:24:58.300 --> 00:25:00.670
and frustrated with
the superficial level

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:03.860
of problem discussion, which
was wasting everyone's time.

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:06.220
And I remember thinking
that no one wanted

00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:08.500
to say or do anything
significant because it was

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:10.607
Friday afternoon,
and to bring up

00:25:10.607 --> 00:25:12.940
a problem at that late day
would mean we'd probably have

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:14.860
to work through the weekend.

00:25:14.860 --> 00:25:16.660
From sheer frustration
about my time

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:18.580
being wasted and
knowing that I was

00:25:18.580 --> 00:25:20.920
going to be the one working
the problem anyways,

00:25:20.920 --> 00:25:23.050
I spoke up and
interrupted the meeting,

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:25.060
used a simple demonstration
with the parts

00:25:25.060 --> 00:25:28.610
to show what was wrong, and
suggested how to fix it.

00:25:28.610 --> 00:25:29.350
I was right.

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:30.820
We worked the weekend.

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:32.500
But we fixed the
design, and were back

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:35.470
by testing by midweek,
and it worked just fine.

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:38.180
My immediate supervision was
very pleased with my efforts.

00:25:38.180 --> 00:25:40.660
But the chief engineer
was not pleased at all

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:43.120
for me exposing the problem
at the meeting in front

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:44.350
of the customer.

00:25:44.350 --> 00:25:47.140
Approximately a year later,
the same chief engineer

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:48.700
tried in vain to
order me to give

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:51.340
a slanted technical
presentation to the Air Force

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:53.260
on the same missile structure.

00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:54.799
I refused to do so.

00:25:54.799 --> 00:25:57.340
And the chief engineer gave the
presentation himself, with me

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:59.890
in the room, knowing
full well that if I

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:02.620
was asked any detailed
questions about my analysis,

00:26:02.620 --> 00:26:04.820
that I would have shot
him down in flames.

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:08.290
I have never seen anybody
sweat so much in all my life

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:12.390
as that man did during
his presentation.

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:15.300
I'm positive that that
confrontation and refusal

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:16.950
to make that
presentation cost me

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:19.800
a supervisor's position
at a later date

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:21.840
because he knew he
could not manipulate me

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:24.280
as he was accustomed
to doing with others.

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:27.960
The lessons learned from
these experiences at Celesco

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:31.340
was the opportunity to
practice past lessons learned

00:26:31.340 --> 00:26:34.590
and to reinforce my ethical
professional behavior.

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:37.520
In 1977, I found
myself at my 11th job

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:40.680
at Garrett AiResearch
in Torrance, California.

00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:43.680
While working as a contract
engineer-- and some of you

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:46.010
may know that term
as a job shopper,

00:26:46.010 --> 00:26:48.080
where a company
actually shops you

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:51.331
in to a parent company that
needs some temporary expert

00:26:51.331 --> 00:26:51.830
help.

00:26:54.520 --> 00:26:56.500
While going in there
as a contract engineer,

00:26:56.500 --> 00:26:58.060
I discovered after
approximately one

00:26:58.060 --> 00:27:00.520
month that a structural
beam designed

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:03.910
to support a 50,000
pound heat exchanger

00:27:03.910 --> 00:27:05.285
would fail in
lateral instability

00:27:05.285 --> 00:27:08.800
if loaded during assembly,
and could cause loss of life

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:11.119
to those that were
involved in the assembling.

00:27:11.119 --> 00:27:13.660
I was aware that the equipment
had been designed and analyzed

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:15.670
during the preceding
1 and 1/2 years,

00:27:15.670 --> 00:27:17.750
by engineers with
advanced degrees,

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:19.990
and was in the final
detail drawing phase

00:27:19.990 --> 00:27:21.790
prior to fabrication.

00:27:21.790 --> 00:27:25.120
I told my supervisor about my
analysis predicting failure,

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:26.980
but he was somewhat skeptical.

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:30.390
And so I thought, oh,
no, here I go again--

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:32.890
another confrontation.

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:34.870
I arrived for work the
next day, and had barely

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:37.450
sat down at my desk,
when my supervisor asked

00:27:37.450 --> 00:27:40.240
me to go with him to the
chief engineer's office.

00:27:40.240 --> 00:27:43.570
I was surprised and,
initially, pleased to find that

00:27:43.570 --> 00:27:47.590
the prior evening, they had
built a balsa wood beam, scale

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:49.750
model beam, but
disappointed to learn

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:53.860
that its purpose was to prove
me, the outsider, wrong.

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:55.570
We loaded the beam in
the lab and plotted

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:57.460
deflection versus load
curves as the beam

00:27:57.460 --> 00:27:59.020
was incrementally loaded.

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:01.180
And as soon as the
critical load was reached,

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:04.690
the beam started to roll
and deflect laterally--

00:28:04.690 --> 00:28:08.680
much to the astonishment of my
supervisor and chief engineer.

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:10.570
They quickly asked me
for my recommendations

00:28:10.570 --> 00:28:11.770
for fixing the beam.

00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:13.240
And I responded
the fix was simply

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:17.140
to place a closure plate on
the open side of the beam

00:28:17.140 --> 00:28:18.370
on the compression side.

00:28:18.370 --> 00:28:20.770
And this would prevent the
lateral instability action

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:22.090
from occurring.

00:28:22.090 --> 00:28:24.460
A strip of balsa wood
simulating that closure plate

00:28:24.460 --> 00:28:26.590
was glued in place
and allowed to dry.

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:29.260
The beam was again loaded
in the same load sequence

00:28:29.260 --> 00:28:31.720
as before to approximately
twice the load,

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.500
without any signs of
lateral instability.

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:36.700
I was thanked for finding
and correcting the problem

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:38.940
and, about a week
later, offered a chance

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:41.740
to go work direct for
Garrett Corporation

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:44.620
after my contract expired
with the job shop.

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:46.270
The offer of direct
employment is

00:28:46.270 --> 00:28:49.360
very significant to this
example in experience,

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:53.020
since the engineering department
was primarily composed of PhDs,

00:28:53.020 --> 00:28:56.470
with only a few holding master's
degrees, and to my knowledge,

00:28:56.470 --> 00:28:57.970
no one in the
engineering department

00:28:57.970 --> 00:29:00.100
had a Bachelor of
Science degree.

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:02.650
When I went to personnel
with my completed application

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:06.010
form and resume and asked to
be processed into the company,

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:09.170
the man became very
upset about three things.

00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:12.230
First, according to him,
I had held 10 jobs before,

00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:15.010
and he called me a
transient employee.

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:16.750
Second, he scoffed
at me for having

00:29:16.750 --> 00:29:19.040
a Bachelor of Science degree.

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:21.710
And third, there was no
outstanding requisition

00:29:21.710 --> 00:29:24.680
in the engineering
department for a new hire.

00:29:24.680 --> 00:29:27.620
He told me point-blank that
had I applied for employment

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:29.420
through the front
door, my resume

00:29:29.420 --> 00:29:31.190
would have been
swiftly discarded

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:34.100
without any chance of
getting an interview.

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:36.650
At that point, I began to
resent his attitude towards me,

00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:39.890
and told him to either
process me in, or I would--

00:29:39.890 --> 00:29:42.290
directly, or I would return
to the chief engineer,

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:44.940
and tell him he was abusing me.

00:29:44.940 --> 00:29:46.920
And since he didn't
want-- and I assumed,

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:49.140
he didn't want me to do that.

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:51.740
After my statement, he
never spoke another word.

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:54.630
And I probably hold the
record for the shortest time

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:58.830
to process a new hire
into Garrett Corporation.

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:01.200
Lessons learned
from this action was

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:04.320
that the good guys do
win, occasionally, when

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:07.730
managers have the courage to
listen and act upon the facts.

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:08.432
And they did.

00:30:08.432 --> 00:30:10.390
Even though they were
trying to prove me wrong,

00:30:10.390 --> 00:30:12.360
they at least listened,
and took the time

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:15.120
to find out if there
was something wrong.

00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:16.590
And the other
lesson I learned is

00:30:16.590 --> 00:30:21.767
job shopping is an absolutely
fantastic way to find a job.

00:30:21.767 --> 00:30:24.100
But you can only do that after
you have about five years

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:26.036
experience, unfortunately.

00:30:26.036 --> 00:30:30.520
In July 1980, I found myself
at my 14th and planned

00:30:30.520 --> 00:30:33.880
last industrial job
at Morton-Thiokol.

00:30:33.880 --> 00:30:36.040
This was planned to be
my retirement position,

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:38.350
since we moved to a
beautiful, but rather remote,

00:30:38.350 --> 00:30:39.960
area of northern Utah.

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:42.460
If you didn't work for
Thiokol in engineering,

00:30:42.460 --> 00:30:43.420
you didn't work at all.

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:45.750
It was that simple.

00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:47.580
The major point I
want to convey to you

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:50.760
is my conduct on the job,
predating my deep involvement

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:52.530
with the escalation
of the space shuttle

00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:55.050
booster joint O-ring problems.

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:57.720
I conducted myself as I had
always done in the past.

00:30:57.720 --> 00:31:01.740
And I soon found myself involved
in many of Thiokol's problems,

00:31:01.740 --> 00:31:04.260
both on and off the
shuttle program.

00:31:04.260 --> 00:31:06.750
I talked with many people
about known problems,

00:31:06.750 --> 00:31:10.410
and provided recommendations
for hardware modifications,

00:31:10.410 --> 00:31:13.200
process changes, quality
inspection techniques,

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.520
analysis methods, and so forth.

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:17.790
But I met with totally
unexpected resistance,

00:31:17.790 --> 00:31:20.490
and was told straight out,
that they all knew best

00:31:20.490 --> 00:31:22.680
how to do things, since
they had been doing

00:31:22.680 --> 00:31:25.200
it the same way for 20 years.

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:28.490
I sometimes replied
from pure frustration

00:31:28.490 --> 00:31:30.240
that their methods
were based on two years

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:33.570
experience multiplied
10 times, and that they

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:37.580
were 20 years behind the rest
of the industrialized world.

00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:40.130
Since no one will listen to
my verbal recommendations,

00:31:40.130 --> 00:31:42.860
I started writing memos, and
followed the same procedures

00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:45.230
I have already outlined for you.

00:31:45.230 --> 00:31:48.860
I soon became recognized
by management as a doer,

00:31:48.860 --> 00:31:52.160
and became somewhat of a
resident expert on fasteners,

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:55.490
material selection, ground
support equipment, and failure

00:31:55.490 --> 00:31:56.000
analysis.

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:58.640
So managers outside
the shuttle program

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:01.220
sought my help with
their problems.

00:32:01.220 --> 00:32:03.140
My relationship within
the shuttle program

00:32:03.140 --> 00:32:05.930
still remained
somewhat adversarial,

00:32:05.930 --> 00:32:08.850
especially with the director
of the engineering department.

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:11.180
I had gone to that
director many, many times

00:32:11.180 --> 00:32:15.060
with good recommendations,
which he acknowledged as good,

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:18.080
but then he always took
the business as usual side

00:32:18.080 --> 00:32:19.420
against me.

00:32:19.420 --> 00:32:22.580
Approximately 3 and 1/2 years
into that mode of operation,

00:32:22.580 --> 00:32:25.610
I was given a promotion to
project engineering manager

00:32:25.610 --> 00:32:28.050
on the shuttle booster
case structure.

00:32:28.050 --> 00:32:30.320
This is very
significant because I

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:32.480
leaped over several
supervisors who had been

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:35.150
with Thiokol over 20 years.

00:32:35.150 --> 00:32:38.150
And it demonstrates that the
principles of professionalism

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:40.730
and ethical conduct that
I've been explaining to you

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:43.050
can indeed be rewarded.

00:32:43.050 --> 00:32:45.140
I hope this mix of my
personal experiences

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:48.230
demonstrates to all of you,
both within and outside

00:32:48.230 --> 00:32:50.540
of engineering, that
a rewarding career is

00:32:50.540 --> 00:32:53.540
possible to attain
within the framework

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:55.700
of ethical
professionalism, even when

00:32:55.700 --> 00:32:59.000
employed by organizations
that conduct themselves

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:01.560
in a somewhat unethical manner.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:05.960
Remember, as long as management
can use your expertise,

00:33:05.960 --> 00:33:08.870
and still control
their overall agenda,

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:11.510
there is little danger
of serious retribution,

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:13.280
unless you pin them
in a corner like I

00:33:13.280 --> 00:33:16.440
did during the
Challenger investigation.

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:19.400
I also hope you realize that
all your future decisions will

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:22.130
primarily be based
upon the character

00:33:22.130 --> 00:33:25.430
of your previous decisions
on small and relatively

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:29.370
unimportant matters during
your careers up to that point.

00:33:29.370 --> 00:33:31.980
By way of example, and
with absolutely no intent

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:34.800
towards malice, I
wasn't surprised

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:37.770
when the vice president of
engineering at Morton-Thiokol

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:39.930
capitulated to the general
manager's directive

00:33:39.930 --> 00:33:44.100
to take off his engineering hat
and put on his management hat

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:48.100
at the meeting prior to
the Challenger launch.

00:33:48.100 --> 00:33:50.560
I had observed this man
in several different

00:33:50.560 --> 00:33:53.320
organizational positions
for 5 and 1/2 years

00:33:53.320 --> 00:33:56.260
conduct himself in a
manner to always side

00:33:56.260 --> 00:33:59.710
with the business as usual
management philosophy.

00:33:59.710 --> 00:34:00.730
I wasn't surprised.

00:34:00.730 --> 00:34:03.130
But I sure as hell
was angry with him

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:06.310
for not standing up for what he
knew was technically correct,

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:09.460
as evidenced by his own
conclusions and recommendations

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:13.510
chart that he presented in the
first part of that telecon.

00:34:13.510 --> 00:34:16.560
My closing remarks
are very simple.

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:20.730
I implore all of you who are
pursuing engineering careers,

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:23.670
to please set a goal,
and take, and pass

00:34:23.670 --> 00:34:27.389
the engineering in training
exam prior to graduation.

00:34:27.389 --> 00:34:30.150
Then after five years of
engineering experience,

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:33.719
take the P exam and become a
licensed professional engineer,

00:34:33.719 --> 00:34:36.460
whether your chosen
field requires it or not.

00:34:36.460 --> 00:34:38.670
I do not say these
things lightly.

00:34:38.670 --> 00:34:40.010
I did it the hard way.

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:42.300
30 years out of
school last October,

00:34:42.300 --> 00:34:46.420
I took the EIT exam on Thursday,
and the P exam on Friday.

00:34:46.420 --> 00:34:48.780
And if I can do it,
and I passed them,

00:34:48.780 --> 00:34:50.880
at this institution with
the quality of education

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:55.020
you are getting, it ought to be
a piece of cake for you people.

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:56.730
Join and become an
active participant

00:34:56.730 --> 00:34:59.220
in a professional society,
and start a movement

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:02.400
to change the societies towards
support for their members,

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:04.140
as opposed to
supporting the industry

00:35:04.140 --> 00:35:08.160
agenda of using employees
as a renewable resource.

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:10.760
Please don't join the
business as usual gang.

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:13.440
And always remember,
you have three choices--

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:17.740
exit, voice, and loyalty.

00:35:17.740 --> 00:35:20.630
But I hope you won't
subjectively choose loyalty

00:35:20.630 --> 00:35:22.400
to preserve your status quo.

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:23.645
Thank you very much.

00:35:23.645 --> 00:35:27.110
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:49.385 --> 00:35:51.860
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:35:54.335 --> 00:35:56.590
MODERATOR: OK, we'll
have brief remarks

00:35:56.590 --> 00:35:58.890
from each of the
panelists, and then we'll

00:35:58.890 --> 00:35:59.960
have open discussion.

00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:02.870
You'll notice there's--
there are mics on the floor.

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:08.690
Our first speaker
will be Freada Klein.

00:36:08.690 --> 00:36:11.270
KLEIN: I want to comment
on a few of the things

00:36:11.270 --> 00:36:13.790
that Roger talked
about in terms of how

00:36:13.790 --> 00:36:17.960
to evaluate a company's
ethical practices, approaches

00:36:17.960 --> 00:36:20.510
to ethical issues.

00:36:20.510 --> 00:36:23.420
I think he focused
on issues of--

00:36:23.420 --> 00:36:25.880
related to safety,
related to health,

00:36:25.880 --> 00:36:31.760
related to possible dire
consequences for the end-user.

00:36:31.760 --> 00:36:34.850
I think there are also other
kinds of ethical dilemmas

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:37.760
that arise in day-to-day
work lives of scientists

00:36:37.760 --> 00:36:39.590
and engineers.

00:36:39.590 --> 00:36:42.260
Some of those have to do with
how employees are treated

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:47.020
and how employees treat
each other as well.

00:36:47.020 --> 00:36:51.170
There are two categories
of ethical issues

00:36:51.170 --> 00:36:53.780
that I think are
important to distinguish.

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:59.210
One is ethical issues which
are those of omission, where

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:01.770
information is left
out, or where people,

00:37:01.770 --> 00:37:04.730
or where groups are excluded.

00:37:04.730 --> 00:37:06.650
And the other would
be ethical issues

00:37:06.650 --> 00:37:10.340
which are [? commission, ?]
where there are actual fraud

00:37:10.340 --> 00:37:14.630
or problems committed.

00:37:14.630 --> 00:37:18.890
When looking at a company,
I think Roger's point

00:37:18.890 --> 00:37:21.500
about looking at where
the QC manager reports,

00:37:21.500 --> 00:37:26.640
and whether or not there's
a ombuds is very important,

00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:27.980
and where they report.

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:33.590
There ought to, also, be an
array of complaint mechanisms.

00:37:33.590 --> 00:37:36.440
There ought to be formal
complaint mechanisms that

00:37:36.440 --> 00:37:39.440
have a peer review component.

00:37:39.440 --> 00:37:43.730
And there ought to be informal
complaint structures that

00:37:43.730 --> 00:37:47.090
assure confidentiality.

00:37:47.090 --> 00:37:52.310
I think it's also worth
asking about the folklore.

00:37:52.310 --> 00:37:55.914
What are people rewarded
for in that organization?

00:37:55.914 --> 00:37:56.705
Who are the heroes?

00:37:56.705 --> 00:37:58.130
Who are the heroines?

00:37:58.130 --> 00:38:02.570
What are the heroic
acts in recent memory

00:38:02.570 --> 00:38:03.930
in the organization?

00:38:03.930 --> 00:38:05.935
Will tell you a lot
about what's valued--

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:14.390
if there's an open door
policy, for example.

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:17.720
Many IBM employees will tell
you that the open door hits you

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:20.390
in the rear on the way out.

00:38:20.390 --> 00:38:24.400
One local high tech
company has a phrase well

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:26.240
known amongst its
employees called

00:38:26.240 --> 00:38:30.860
CLMs, Career Limiting Moves.

00:38:30.860 --> 00:38:34.580
Any place has part of
an informal culture that

00:38:34.580 --> 00:38:36.940
will tell you about its values.

00:38:36.940 --> 00:38:39.950
And those are worth finding
out before signing on.

00:38:42.506 --> 00:38:44.130
MODERATOR: Thank you
very much, Freada.

00:38:44.130 --> 00:38:45.390
Jim, would you like to speak?

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:47.389
Jim Melcher from
electrical engineering.

00:38:47.389 --> 00:38:49.430
MELCHER: Yes, I'm not sure
I have so much in mind

00:38:49.430 --> 00:38:52.400
here, giving any
kind of presentation,

00:38:52.400 --> 00:38:54.330
so much as asking
some questions.

00:38:54.330 --> 00:38:58.820
I had the good fortune yesterday
of spending a couple of hours

00:38:58.820 --> 00:39:00.620
in Carolyn's home with Roger.

00:39:00.620 --> 00:39:05.540
And I'd like to, I think, maybe
get some reactions from you

00:39:05.540 --> 00:39:06.290
on some points.

00:39:09.100 --> 00:39:11.770
One of these has to do with
the continued theme that

00:39:11.770 --> 00:39:14.530
came across of your pride,
and your professionalism,

00:39:14.530 --> 00:39:17.770
your ability to be
a good engineer.

00:39:17.770 --> 00:39:19.690
Let me put this in our context.

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:21.820
In this room, in
a faculty meeting

00:39:21.820 --> 00:39:25.240
last spring, I guess, now,
we had a wide ranging debate

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:27.850
on the subject of pass-fail.

00:39:27.850 --> 00:39:31.420
And you could imagine, this is
a grading system for freshmen.

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:33.880
And it has all sorts
of sides to it,

00:39:33.880 --> 00:39:38.920
but you'd find that perhaps half
of the engineering community

00:39:38.920 --> 00:39:41.320
was very much in favor
of seeing it dropped.

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:45.790
They regarded it as a
mechanism of compromising

00:39:45.790 --> 00:39:48.270
the expertise of our students.

00:39:48.270 --> 00:39:54.190
Now, out of that, I would gather
they probably didn't succeed,

00:39:54.190 --> 00:39:55.480
really, in making their point.

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:59.470
But that the point was, as I saw
it, that if a person were going

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:03.400
to take a strong position,
as you have many times,

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:08.450
it had to be based on being
very good at what you did.

00:40:08.450 --> 00:40:13.265
You, I gather, feel
somewhat along those lines?

00:40:13.265 --> 00:40:14.140
BOISJOLY: I think so.

00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:19.970
And I-- I guess, the best
thing I can use as an example--

00:40:19.970 --> 00:40:22.400
when I filed my lawsuits
against Morton-Thiokol,

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:26.210
they came down on
me very, very hard,

00:40:26.210 --> 00:40:29.080
trying to discredit me
in every possible manner

00:40:29.080 --> 00:40:31.160
that you can imagine.

00:40:31.160 --> 00:40:34.430
And my simple answer
to some of the things,

00:40:34.430 --> 00:40:37.565
to counter their whole
activity, was simply

00:40:37.565 --> 00:40:40.700
to say that it must be a
horrible reflection on them

00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:43.220
because I was within
$1,000 dollars of doubling

00:40:43.220 --> 00:40:48.830
my salary between 1980
and the summer of 1986.

00:40:48.830 --> 00:40:50.610
And so I rested my case.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:52.190
I said, if I was
a lousy engineer,

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:54.600
a disgruntled engineer,
a know-nothing engineer,

00:40:54.600 --> 00:40:57.740
then what does that make
them for doubling my salary?

00:40:57.740 --> 00:41:00.830
And I also have
to add to that, I

00:41:00.830 --> 00:41:05.695
went through school with a solid
C, not that I'm proud of that.

00:41:05.695 --> 00:41:07.070
If I had to do it
all over again,

00:41:07.070 --> 00:41:09.166
I'd do it much differently.

00:41:09.166 --> 00:41:10.790
But I had a very high
level of interest

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:16.010
in playing tennis, skiing,
and playing hockey.

00:41:16.010 --> 00:41:19.460
And I did do my very best
to keep my grades going.

00:41:19.460 --> 00:41:22.460
But I did not hit my stride
and hit the Dean's List

00:41:22.460 --> 00:41:25.140
until my senior year.

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:26.750
And as a result,
when you've already

00:41:26.750 --> 00:41:28.640
had two years of
solid C work, it's

00:41:28.640 --> 00:41:30.695
very hard to pick up
your grade point average.

00:41:30.695 --> 00:41:32.070
And I probably
wouldn't have been

00:41:32.070 --> 00:41:34.070
allowed in most
universities today,

00:41:34.070 --> 00:41:36.650
if I'd done that same
type of thing today.

00:41:36.650 --> 00:41:39.170
But it doesn't-- the
grades are not in--

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:42.784
I address this relative to
your pass-fail comments.

00:41:42.784 --> 00:41:44.450
The grades don't
necessarily mean you're

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:45.800
going to be a good engineer.

00:41:45.800 --> 00:41:47.300
Grades don't
necessarily mean you're

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:49.258
going to be a person of
integrity, to speak up,

00:41:49.258 --> 00:41:50.210
and do what's right.

00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:55.260
And I use myself as the example.

00:41:55.260 --> 00:41:57.140
I have had no
trouble whatsoever.

00:41:57.140 --> 00:41:59.330
I graduated from Lowell
Technological Institute,

00:41:59.330 --> 00:42:03.500
had 168 credit hours
of course study,

00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:05.930
and where most of my
fellow students coming out

00:42:05.930 --> 00:42:09.230
of the other universities
had between 125 and 135.

00:42:09.230 --> 00:42:12.290
And I've never had any
problem whatsoever facing

00:42:12.290 --> 00:42:15.016
up and matching up across
the industry with anybody.

00:42:15.016 --> 00:42:16.640
And I'll tell you
straight out, there's

00:42:16.640 --> 00:42:19.010
a lot of theoretical
stuff I can't solve.

00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:20.744
But I let the guys
with the PhDs do that.

00:42:20.744 --> 00:42:22.160
There's a lot of
things they can't

00:42:22.160 --> 00:42:25.680
do in putting a product out the
door either, but I can do that.

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:27.997
So together, we can put
out a heck of a product.

00:42:27.997 --> 00:42:29.330
And that's what it really takes.

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:33.470
It takes both types of people
to really integrate, and make

00:42:33.470 --> 00:42:36.750
a company go, and put
out a fantastic product.

00:42:36.750 --> 00:42:38.570
MELCHER: Let me try
and get a bit more

00:42:38.570 --> 00:42:40.100
of the sharing of
the experiences

00:42:40.100 --> 00:42:42.080
you've been through,
maybe following

00:42:42.080 --> 00:42:43.435
a different direction.

00:42:49.740 --> 00:42:52.159
You perhaps could
make an analogy

00:42:52.159 --> 00:42:53.700
to some of the
situations that you've

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:56.219
had with something I saw on
60 Minutes this last Sunday.

00:42:56.219 --> 00:42:57.510
Some of you may have seen this.

00:42:57.510 --> 00:43:01.830
They were interviewing people
whose children had essentially

00:43:01.830 --> 00:43:05.280
drowned, and then
recovered because doctors

00:43:05.280 --> 00:43:07.110
had done a little
bit too good a job,

00:43:07.110 --> 00:43:12.190
and then been permanently
unable to function.

00:43:12.190 --> 00:43:14.610
And they were interviewing
a couple of the mothers who

00:43:14.610 --> 00:43:16.050
were in crucial roles in this.

00:43:16.050 --> 00:43:17.640
And looking back,
these were mothers

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:21.120
who blamed, in a way, the
doctor for having kept

00:43:21.120 --> 00:43:23.760
their child alive when, in
retrospect, they would have

00:43:23.760 --> 00:43:26.850
preferred, really,
albeit a wrenching thing,

00:43:26.850 --> 00:43:29.460
that the child had
been allowed to die.

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:32.590
Now, we make it sound as
though this decision process--

00:43:32.590 --> 00:43:34.950
for example, the one you went
through in only a few days

00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:38.460
around the Challenger incident--
was a very logical progression.

00:43:38.460 --> 00:43:41.420
And I would imagine that there
are events in there where you

00:43:41.420 --> 00:43:43.770
were reacting, just reacting.

00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:46.560
That you were drawing upon
these previous experiences,

00:43:46.560 --> 00:43:48.000
you mentioned, but--

00:43:48.000 --> 00:43:51.360
and your religion, your ethical
view of things, which I wish we

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:54.630
could find some way for
you to share with us more.

00:43:54.630 --> 00:43:58.470
But that there were also
moments, contemplated moments,

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:01.830
when probably you realized
what the consequences were.

00:44:01.830 --> 00:44:04.969
Consequences, again, that aren't
as black and white as we've

00:44:04.969 --> 00:44:05.760
been talking about.

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:08.454
Consequences of walking
the halls in your company

00:44:08.454 --> 00:44:09.870
and finding that
there were people

00:44:09.870 --> 00:44:11.580
who wouldn't speak to you.

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:13.590
The man was the mayor
of a small community.

00:44:13.590 --> 00:44:16.770
And in fact, found
that he was essentially

00:44:16.770 --> 00:44:20.590
shunned, in the Mormon
sense, by the community.

00:44:20.590 --> 00:44:22.200
These are very personal things.

00:44:22.200 --> 00:44:24.533
And there must have been some
point, as this thing began

00:44:24.533 --> 00:44:28.920
to evolve, that you contemplated
what you were getting into,

00:44:28.920 --> 00:44:32.910
and thought about ways that
you could keep your own values,

00:44:32.910 --> 00:44:37.260
and deal with this, and then
took the course you did.

00:44:37.260 --> 00:44:38.947
Is that true?

00:44:38.947 --> 00:44:40.530
BOISJOLY: Yeah, but
it probably didn't

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:42.863
happen as soon in the process
as you might have thought.

00:44:45.650 --> 00:44:49.040
What happened was, from
my past experiences,

00:44:49.040 --> 00:44:53.700
and that's why I emphasized
it in the previous remarks,

00:44:53.700 --> 00:44:57.550
I was the sum total of how
I acted up to that point.

00:44:57.550 --> 00:44:59.940
And it was just natural
for me to continue

00:44:59.940 --> 00:45:02.280
to do what I had always done.

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:05.825
And I did not think of
anything up until the point

00:45:05.825 --> 00:45:09.450
that I got the vilest
looks that you can imagine

00:45:09.450 --> 00:45:12.120
from the general managers--
a general manager

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:13.800
and his associate,
but particularly

00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:14.650
the general manager.

00:45:14.650 --> 00:45:16.470
And myself and Arnie
Thompson compared

00:45:16.470 --> 00:45:17.592
those looks afterwards.

00:45:17.592 --> 00:45:19.800
And those are the looks we
decided you get just prior

00:45:19.800 --> 00:45:21.190
to being fired.

00:45:21.190 --> 00:45:23.970
Now, Arnie was quiet, and
I was quiet at that point

00:45:23.970 --> 00:45:26.460
because there was no way that
we could interject ourselves

00:45:26.460 --> 00:45:28.600
into that management
meeting in the [? caucus. ?]

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:32.240
So that's the first
little indication.

00:45:32.240 --> 00:45:34.795
The next major
thing that happened,

00:45:34.795 --> 00:45:36.170
we just went on
the [? failure ?]

00:45:36.170 --> 00:45:36.961
investigation team.

00:45:36.961 --> 00:45:38.880
And we're doing our
very best to find out

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:41.130
what happened, and
do the very best

00:45:41.130 --> 00:45:43.260
to participate in the redesign.

00:45:43.260 --> 00:45:45.660
But the night
before we were going

00:45:45.660 --> 00:45:48.224
to testify to the Presidential
Commission in closed doors

00:45:48.224 --> 00:45:49.890
was the heighth of
the whole thing, when

00:45:49.890 --> 00:45:52.950
I realized that I was
about to place myself

00:45:52.950 --> 00:45:55.920
right up to my
nostrils in quicksand

00:45:55.920 --> 00:45:59.100
because I had written
in my notebook

00:45:59.100 --> 00:46:01.410
a whole summary of things
that I was going to relate

00:46:01.410 --> 00:46:02.701
to the Presidential Commission.

00:46:02.701 --> 00:46:05.389
And at the end of that, I
said something to the effect--

00:46:05.389 --> 00:46:06.930
and this is in the
National Archives.

00:46:06.930 --> 00:46:08.562
And it is in public
record, but I

00:46:08.562 --> 00:46:10.020
have to paraphrase
'cause I haven't

00:46:10.020 --> 00:46:11.490
looked at it for a long time.

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:13.680
It said something to
the effect that I hope

00:46:13.680 --> 00:46:16.230
and pray that what
I'm about to do

00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:20.730
is not going to ruin my career,
my lifestyle, and my family,

00:46:20.730 --> 00:46:22.500
and then signed
it, and dated it.

00:46:22.500 --> 00:46:24.840
And so that's the first
time, right there,

00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:28.020
that I knew I was
about to plunge myself

00:46:28.020 --> 00:46:31.800
into a potential very
high retribution area.

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:33.660
But I still went
ahead and did it.

00:46:33.660 --> 00:46:37.230
And even afterwards, even with
the anguish from management

00:46:37.230 --> 00:46:40.170
received on the plane back
to the Marshall Space Flight

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:42.760
Center, I still
felt, even with that,

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:45.480
that I still had a chance to
participate because I was being

00:46:45.480 --> 00:46:46.980
told I was going
to have that chance

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:48.750
to participate in a redesign.

00:46:48.750 --> 00:46:51.930
I was very naive, but I truly
believed it in all my heart.

00:46:51.930 --> 00:46:53.550
And I felt that, oh.

00:46:53.550 --> 00:46:55.320
I may have cost myself a raise.

00:46:55.320 --> 00:46:58.440
Or I may cost myself
something here or there.

00:46:58.440 --> 00:47:00.754
It didn't matter, as long
as I was able to participate

00:47:00.754 --> 00:47:01.670
in fixing the problem.

00:47:01.670 --> 00:47:03.750
I was very happy
with what I had done.

00:47:03.750 --> 00:47:05.340
And I'd do it again.

00:47:05.340 --> 00:47:07.110
And then, of course,
later on, I found out

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:08.702
that that was not
to be the case.

00:47:08.702 --> 00:47:10.410
They had actually
given the official word

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:13.830
behind my back to keep
me isolated from Morton--

00:47:13.830 --> 00:47:16.470
from the customer and
the redesign effort

00:47:16.470 --> 00:47:20.400
although I was a technical
participator in giving

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:22.382
information into the system.

00:47:22.382 --> 00:47:24.090
They were never telling
me where it went.

00:47:24.090 --> 00:47:25.920
They changed it sometimes
without my knowledge.

00:47:25.920 --> 00:47:28.211
They were holding meetings
that I didn't know occurred.

00:47:28.211 --> 00:47:29.700
And I got zero feedback.

00:47:29.700 --> 00:47:31.890
So they did, in
fact, isolate me.

00:47:31.890 --> 00:47:33.990
And when I discovered
that, I realized

00:47:33.990 --> 00:47:37.120
just how deep quicksand I
was in at that point in time.

00:47:37.120 --> 00:47:39.900
And then in May, myself,
and the rest of us

00:47:39.900 --> 00:47:41.650
who called ourselves,
at that time,

00:47:41.650 --> 00:47:43.575
the five lepers because
of the ostracization

00:47:43.575 --> 00:47:46.500
that we were getting on
the inside of the company.

00:47:46.500 --> 00:47:49.150
Like he mentioned, people
not facing us as we

00:47:49.150 --> 00:47:52.680
were walking down the corridors,
and other such things.

00:47:52.680 --> 00:47:55.500
We tried to still
turn things around,

00:47:55.500 --> 00:47:57.930
internal to Morton-Thiokol,
because the morale

00:47:57.930 --> 00:47:59.899
was at the basement level
because the guys that

00:47:59.899 --> 00:48:02.190
had made the decision were
still in the same management

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:04.500
organizational positions
that they were in when

00:48:04.500 --> 00:48:06.090
they made that decision.

00:48:06.090 --> 00:48:08.790
And so we asked for a
meeting with the CEO,

00:48:08.790 --> 00:48:12.220
the president of the shuttle
program at that time,

00:48:12.220 --> 00:48:14.790
and the president of
the aerospace operations

00:48:14.790 --> 00:48:16.480
of that company.

00:48:16.480 --> 00:48:17.790
And we failed.

00:48:17.790 --> 00:48:18.660
We basically failed.

00:48:18.660 --> 00:48:24.210
And then that was another stake,
if you will, driven in to me

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:26.440
at that time because
the CEO turned to me.

00:48:26.440 --> 00:48:27.470
He was this close.

00:48:27.470 --> 00:48:30.497
And after my remarks,
he said, son,

00:48:30.497 --> 00:48:32.580
you're never going to get
very far in this company

00:48:32.580 --> 00:48:34.170
with that kind of attitude.

00:48:34.170 --> 00:48:36.480
And all we went to
do was to tell him

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:39.850
that his company was crumbling
around his shoulders.

00:48:39.850 --> 00:48:43.260
And he didn't even know it
because they never took time

00:48:43.260 --> 00:48:45.840
to go down at the employee
level and find out

00:48:45.840 --> 00:48:46.680
how bad things were.

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:48.180
And that's all we
were trying to do,

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:50.130
and yet, that's the
kind of response we got.

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:53.092
At that point in time,
I felt, it's all over.

00:48:53.092 --> 00:48:54.800
And it was just about
a month after that,

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:56.029
that it was indeed all over.

00:48:56.029 --> 00:48:58.320
MELCHER: Well, one thing that
came through in your talk

00:48:58.320 --> 00:49:01.347
was the loyalty you
felt toward, apparently,

00:49:01.347 --> 00:49:03.180
many of the companies,
most of the companies

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:04.650
that you were with.

00:49:04.650 --> 00:49:06.900
I'm wondering if, in fact,
I'm reading something

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:09.090
into your statement
in having the feeling

00:49:09.090 --> 00:49:12.030
that you probably were
motivated by having

00:49:12.030 --> 00:49:14.640
the feeling you were doing the
best thing by your company.

00:49:14.640 --> 00:49:18.090
BOISJOLY: I felt that
night, I felt after it,

00:49:18.090 --> 00:49:22.650
I still feel today, with every
fiber of life in my body,

00:49:22.650 --> 00:49:25.920
that I was a more loyal employee
at Morton-Thiokol and other

00:49:25.920 --> 00:49:28.140
places for doing
what I had done,

00:49:28.140 --> 00:49:32.064
then employees who would turn
their back and say nothing.

00:49:32.064 --> 00:49:33.480
That case about
the missile, there

00:49:33.480 --> 00:49:35.550
were many cases on
that missile that

00:49:35.550 --> 00:49:37.590
were too numerous to mention.

00:49:37.590 --> 00:49:40.200
All the time, I did the
same types of things.

00:49:40.200 --> 00:49:42.390
And I had a very
fruitful career,

00:49:42.390 --> 00:49:44.370
albeit adversarial
at some times,

00:49:44.370 --> 00:49:46.220
but it was fruitful nonetheless.

00:49:46.220 --> 00:49:49.980
And I do, I feel that
you have an obligation

00:49:49.980 --> 00:49:52.140
to be loyal to your
organizational chain

00:49:52.140 --> 00:49:55.380
of command, and
first approach them.

00:49:55.380 --> 00:49:59.010
First, orally, then in
writing, before you ever even

00:49:59.010 --> 00:50:00.520
think about going outside.

00:50:00.520 --> 00:50:02.520
And if you don't do that,
you deserve everything

00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:04.650
you get as an
individual for going

00:50:04.650 --> 00:50:07.500
outside first because you did
not give them the opportunity.

00:50:07.500 --> 00:50:09.270
You owe that company
that loyalty.

00:50:09.270 --> 00:50:10.890
And I have always
felt that, even

00:50:10.890 --> 00:50:13.380
before I read about all
this whistle-blowing stuff.

00:50:13.380 --> 00:50:15.565
And I've always felt
that in my heart.

00:50:15.565 --> 00:50:17.940
I didn't know that I was doing
things right down the line

00:50:17.940 --> 00:50:19.314
like they're
supposed to be done.

00:50:19.314 --> 00:50:21.261
I just felt that that's
the way to do it.

00:50:21.261 --> 00:50:22.620
MELCHER: Thank you.

00:50:22.620 --> 00:50:24.870
MODERATOR: Dave Marks,
[? would you ?] [? like to-- ?]

00:50:24.870 --> 00:50:26.794
Dave Marks from
civil engineering.

00:50:29.490 --> 00:50:30.990
MARKS: I have
questions for you too.

00:50:30.990 --> 00:50:34.834
It's better to ask
questions than to receive.

00:50:34.834 --> 00:50:37.320
But it seems to me that the
civil engineers are a bit

00:50:37.320 --> 00:50:42.360
different than other engineers.

00:50:42.360 --> 00:50:44.610
Basic definition that I've
heard of civil engineers

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:46.710
is they build very large
things that don't move,

00:50:46.710 --> 00:50:51.000
and you want to make sure
that they don't move,

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:53.580
implying safety considerations.

00:50:53.580 --> 00:50:55.880
And I've rarely seen
a civil engineer

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:57.630
who gets into trouble
because he brings up

00:50:57.630 --> 00:51:00.212
a safety consideration.

00:51:00.212 --> 00:51:01.920
I mean, the earliest
part of our training

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:03.960
is watching a film loop
of the Tacoma Narrows

00:51:03.960 --> 00:51:05.880
Bridge, the Galloping Gertie.

00:51:05.880 --> 00:51:07.890
And they show that to
you over, over again.

00:51:07.890 --> 00:51:12.510
And the message is don't
let this happen to you.

00:51:12.510 --> 00:51:15.255
But in fact, people who
work in the public sector,

00:51:15.255 --> 00:51:18.270
or in public sector engineering
run into different types

00:51:18.270 --> 00:51:20.220
of ethical issues.

00:51:20.220 --> 00:51:25.710
And the basic ethical issues
are really relate to values

00:51:25.710 --> 00:51:28.740
because very often
you are, in fact,

00:51:28.740 --> 00:51:32.350
society's engineers and society
does not speak with one voice.

00:51:32.350 --> 00:51:35.340
Society speaks in a
very confusing way.

00:51:35.340 --> 00:51:36.840
And there are major
parts of society

00:51:36.840 --> 00:51:39.390
that, in fact, don't
have a voice at all.

00:51:39.390 --> 00:51:41.610
And the questions
that I'll raise to you

00:51:41.610 --> 00:51:44.950
have to do between this
dichotomy between these things,

00:51:44.950 --> 00:51:47.640
I mean, particularly in
the environmental area.

00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:51.030
Or for instance, take the
transportation area, and all

00:51:51.030 --> 00:51:53.190
of our young engineers
can design the bridges

00:51:53.190 --> 00:51:56.040
and pavements of an
interstate highway system,

00:51:56.040 --> 00:51:59.646
yet has anybody ever
asked about the decision

00:51:59.646 --> 00:52:01.020
for an interstate
highway system,

00:52:01.020 --> 00:52:03.690
and how that has changed
the basic fabric of America?

00:52:03.690 --> 00:52:05.850
I mean, it really has.

00:52:05.850 --> 00:52:09.270
When we look at the Superfund
cleanup, which is, of course,

00:52:09.270 --> 00:52:10.440
tremendously interest to us.

00:52:10.440 --> 00:52:14.010
No one wants to see hazardous
and toxic wastes around.

00:52:14.010 --> 00:52:17.040
In fact, we have
been unable, so far,

00:52:17.040 --> 00:52:18.990
to define how clean is clean.

00:52:18.990 --> 00:52:21.060
We simply don't have the
scientific knowledge.

00:52:21.060 --> 00:52:23.520
Nonetheless, we can't wait
until we have that knowledge

00:52:23.520 --> 00:52:26.260
to continue.

00:52:26.260 --> 00:52:31.380
And so we have tried to bring
a veneer of quantification in.

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:33.910
Something is clean,
and something

00:52:33.910 --> 00:52:36.970
that's slightly larger
than that is not clean.

00:52:36.970 --> 00:52:39.465
We have a whole
ethic built up now

00:52:39.465 --> 00:52:42.750
of doing environmental impact
statements for new procedures,

00:52:42.750 --> 00:52:45.390
which somehow is supposed to
represent a valid decision

00:52:45.390 --> 00:52:47.790
process, yet a
young employee might

00:52:47.790 --> 00:52:49.890
question how well it does.

00:52:49.890 --> 00:52:52.230
We have the growing of
environmental terrorists, who

00:52:52.230 --> 00:52:55.776
have gotten tired of the way
environmental things are going,

00:52:55.776 --> 00:52:57.900
and are simply taking
matters into their own hands,

00:52:57.900 --> 00:53:00.930
much in the same way of
animal rights activists.

00:53:00.930 --> 00:53:04.470
So I guess the two questions I
would ask you in this context,

00:53:04.470 --> 00:53:08.300
and really come back to
your question, this is--

00:53:08.300 --> 00:53:12.900
is how does one deal in
this dichotomy of lining up

00:53:12.900 --> 00:53:15.990
your own personal values
with society's values,

00:53:15.990 --> 00:53:18.240
when they're not
well-represented?

00:53:18.240 --> 00:53:22.015
And the second question is, I
would come back to a question

00:53:22.015 --> 00:53:23.640
that you raised, what
is the definition

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:29.130
of a rewarding career, when
technical analysis, and being

00:53:29.130 --> 00:53:32.280
good in technical analysis on
certain parts of the problem,

00:53:32.280 --> 00:53:34.710
often does not address
the whole problem?

00:53:34.710 --> 00:53:37.210
BOISJOLY: Well,
OK, first, let me

00:53:37.210 --> 00:53:40.740
have a little preamble to the
difference between your area

00:53:40.740 --> 00:53:43.080
and the area that
I functioned in.

00:53:43.080 --> 00:53:45.480
We have a awful
paradox that exists

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:47.820
in this country at this time.

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:50.160
The people who build
our bridges, our roads,

00:53:50.160 --> 00:53:52.050
and our buildings are
the civil engineers

00:53:52.050 --> 00:53:54.240
who are licensed engineers.

00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:55.720
They have a code of ethics.

00:53:55.720 --> 00:53:57.362
They have backing.

00:53:57.362 --> 00:53:59.820
They have all the things that
we don't have in the industry

00:53:59.820 --> 00:54:00.660
that I was in.

00:54:00.660 --> 00:54:02.984
The people that build
your automobiles that

00:54:02.984 --> 00:54:04.650
go over those roads,
the planes that you

00:54:04.650 --> 00:54:07.650
fly in on a daily basis,
which I was involved in,

00:54:07.650 --> 00:54:10.530
the missiles, and the
spacecraft are not

00:54:10.530 --> 00:54:12.130
licensed people by and large.

00:54:12.130 --> 00:54:14.400
You don't have to be,
and so most aren't.

00:54:14.400 --> 00:54:16.560
Right there is part
of the root cause

00:54:16.560 --> 00:54:19.770
of why I suggest everybody to
become licensed, whether it's

00:54:19.770 --> 00:54:21.930
required or not,
because I firmly believe

00:54:21.930 --> 00:54:24.090
that you could pull that in.

00:54:24.090 --> 00:54:31.090
As far as a rewarding
career is concerned,

00:54:31.090 --> 00:54:35.830
I defined my career
as rewarding because I

00:54:35.830 --> 00:54:39.300
was able to make a lot
of corrective actions.

00:54:39.300 --> 00:54:41.760
I was able to earn
a very good living.

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:44.150
I was very able
and willing to tell

00:54:44.150 --> 00:54:47.010
them to stick it in their
ear when it became an impasse

00:54:47.010 --> 00:54:48.750
and leave.

00:54:48.750 --> 00:54:51.180
Sometimes I changed-- it
was a personal decision that

00:54:51.180 --> 00:54:52.980
had nothing to do with the job.

00:54:52.980 --> 00:54:55.870
But I felt I had
the opportunity,

00:54:55.870 --> 00:54:59.040
in a general sense, in
a high sense, actually,

00:54:59.040 --> 00:55:02.220
in the 27 years, to participate
in a reasonably high level

00:55:02.220 --> 00:55:05.940
of engineering activity, and
to do the very best I could,

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:08.130
whether they wanted me to
participate in that manner

00:55:08.130 --> 00:55:08.629
or not.

00:55:08.629 --> 00:55:11.910
And so for me, by my
personal definition,

00:55:11.910 --> 00:55:13.830
I was allowed to do
most of the things

00:55:13.830 --> 00:55:16.500
that I was able to do well
because I forced the issue

00:55:16.500 --> 00:55:18.220
to do it that way.

00:55:18.220 --> 00:55:22.380
I often found myself
wandering in the shop,

00:55:22.380 --> 00:55:24.810
often found myself wandering
out in the test lab,

00:55:24.810 --> 00:55:27.750
often found myself in
places where people like

00:55:27.750 --> 00:55:30.510
me don't normally
go, and so I made

00:55:30.510 --> 00:55:33.049
it rewarding in the sense that
I did those types of things.

00:55:33.049 --> 00:55:35.340
And that's one of the reasons
I recommended to students

00:55:35.340 --> 00:55:37.950
to do the same types
of things because you

00:55:37.950 --> 00:55:42.180
can get the whole circle
closed of experience.

00:55:42.180 --> 00:55:45.870
You get to, not only look
at the specifications,

00:55:45.870 --> 00:55:50.160
design, and analyze, test,
go out in shop see it built,

00:55:50.160 --> 00:55:52.020
and then follow up
with the customer.

00:55:52.020 --> 00:55:54.180
But if you only focus
on the one area,

00:55:54.180 --> 00:55:58.260
you don't get that satisfaction,
that feedback loop,

00:55:58.260 --> 00:56:02.280
and that next step of
experience to carry

00:56:02.280 --> 00:56:04.470
on to the next place
or the next project.

00:56:04.470 --> 00:56:06.490
And so that's how I define
the rewarding career.

00:56:06.490 --> 00:56:08.062
Now, the first part
of your question,

00:56:08.062 --> 00:56:09.270
you'll have to repeat for me.

00:56:09.270 --> 00:56:11.460
But I'm not sure, I have to--

00:56:11.460 --> 00:56:13.946
I can grasp the
total significance

00:56:13.946 --> 00:56:14.820
what you were asking.

00:56:14.820 --> 00:56:17.630
MARKS: I guess once
one moves beyond safety

00:56:17.630 --> 00:56:19.352
and into another set of values.

00:56:19.352 --> 00:56:21.310
For instance, there are
engineers who work on--

00:56:21.310 --> 00:56:22.119
BOISJOLY: Oh, OK.

00:56:22.119 --> 00:56:23.910
MARKS: --who work on
transportation systems

00:56:23.910 --> 00:56:26.160
and, technically,
design them very well.

00:56:26.160 --> 00:56:28.710
But in fact, they're not--

00:56:28.710 --> 00:56:30.990
they're-- in the process,
they're changing the basic

00:56:30.990 --> 00:56:32.280
fabric of America.

00:56:32.280 --> 00:56:36.450
Engineers who are working on
environmental impact statements

00:56:36.450 --> 00:56:38.580
doing wonderful
models of groundwater,

00:56:38.580 --> 00:56:41.550
but, in fact, missing
other parts of the problem

00:56:41.550 --> 00:56:44.370
because one can't
do analysis on that.

00:56:44.370 --> 00:56:47.760
There are major
non-technical value issues.

00:56:47.760 --> 00:56:51.010
I've seen major reservoir
systems designed

00:56:51.010 --> 00:56:53.970
in developing
countries, which will

00:56:53.970 --> 00:56:55.942
involve moving
millions of people

00:56:55.942 --> 00:56:57.150
just to build the reservoirs.

00:56:57.150 --> 00:57:00.050
And these people at the lowest
economic level, and they will--

00:57:00.050 --> 00:57:04.050
their complete societal
fabric will be disrupted.

00:57:04.050 --> 00:57:06.540
No one speaks for those people.

00:57:06.540 --> 00:57:09.610
And to a certain extent,
a young engineer,

00:57:09.610 --> 00:57:12.650
were he to try and
speak for those people,

00:57:12.650 --> 00:57:15.240
or to address those large
issues, and has no form,

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:17.760
has no ability,
it's not part of--

00:57:17.760 --> 00:57:19.980
according to people, it's
not part of engineering.

00:57:19.980 --> 00:57:22.210
There's no way to
make that argument.

00:57:22.210 --> 00:57:26.112
So how does one work in
a situation like that?

00:57:26.112 --> 00:57:26.890
Ha, gingerly.

00:57:26.890 --> 00:57:30.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:30.030 --> 00:57:32.355
BOISJOLY: I honestly don't know.

00:57:32.355 --> 00:57:35.470
It's like you say, there are no
mechanisms for that to occur.

00:57:35.470 --> 00:57:37.750
And even if there
was a tiny mechanism,

00:57:37.750 --> 00:57:40.970
it's not a significant
mechanism for the big picture

00:57:40.970 --> 00:57:42.450
to allow it to occur.

00:57:42.450 --> 00:57:45.420
You end up becoming a
majority of one, usually.

00:57:45.420 --> 00:57:47.760
I don't know how to
answer that question.

00:57:50.480 --> 00:57:55.400
MARKS: Do you think
that young engineers,

00:57:55.400 --> 00:57:58.070
that there's something missing
in their educational process

00:57:58.070 --> 00:58:03.950
that makes them afraid to
stand up in tough situations?

00:58:03.950 --> 00:58:06.500
Or is it a sense of values?

00:58:06.500 --> 00:58:10.070
I mean, why is it that you had
bosses who, in fact, wanted

00:58:10.070 --> 00:58:12.210
to cut the line?

00:58:12.210 --> 00:58:14.210
Who wanted-- who knew
something was wrong,

00:58:14.210 --> 00:58:16.370
but wanted to cover it up?

00:58:16.370 --> 00:58:18.320
Is that an educational problem?

00:58:18.320 --> 00:58:22.914
Or is it something about how
people move up in systems?

00:58:22.914 --> 00:58:24.830
BOISJOLY: I think it's
an educational problem,

00:58:24.830 --> 00:58:26.730
but not in the sense
that you view it.

00:58:26.730 --> 00:58:30.710
It's not what you're taught, but
what the people have observed.

00:58:30.710 --> 00:58:35.210
Our government and our companies
have done a marvelous job

00:58:35.210 --> 00:58:39.050
in this country of making sure
that everybody in this country

00:58:39.050 --> 00:58:42.890
knows that when you stand
up for a cause that's right,

00:58:42.890 --> 00:58:44.990
you're going to get creamed.

00:58:44.990 --> 00:58:46.940
And they may-- that's
the first thing they do.

00:58:46.940 --> 00:58:51.230
If you think back, you think
back in every visible thing

00:58:51.230 --> 00:58:54.290
that you've read
about in recent times,

00:58:54.290 --> 00:58:57.020
and not one company
or one agency

00:58:57.020 --> 00:59:00.610
has ever addressed the issue
for which has been brought out.

00:59:00.610 --> 00:59:02.720
They've always
addressed the person,

00:59:02.720 --> 00:59:05.000
and have absolutely
decimated the person,

00:59:05.000 --> 00:59:07.390
and never discussed the issue.

00:59:07.390 --> 00:59:10.670
And so the message comes home,
just by reading about it,

00:59:10.670 --> 00:59:14.990
that you're going to pay a
very bad price in the norm,

00:59:14.990 --> 00:59:16.590
if you go and do
something like this.

00:59:16.590 --> 00:59:20.570
And so it becomes a focal
point in many people's lives.

00:59:20.570 --> 00:59:25.190
My boss, and Arnie Thompson's
boss was the same man,

00:59:25.190 --> 00:59:27.440
he is a registered
professional engineer.

00:59:27.440 --> 00:59:29.690
He sat in those meetings.

00:59:29.690 --> 00:59:33.350
He went to Washington and heard
the Presidential Commission

00:59:33.350 --> 00:59:34.940
hearings along with us.

00:59:34.940 --> 00:59:38.330
He never said a word in the
meetings before the launch.

00:59:38.330 --> 00:59:43.370
He never said a word to the
Presidential Commission ever.

00:59:43.370 --> 00:59:46.010
If I had had a license,
I think, and I hoped,

00:59:46.010 --> 00:59:48.290
I would have gone a step
further and threatened them

00:59:48.290 --> 00:59:50.680
with my code of ethics.

00:59:50.680 --> 00:59:53.470
And that's one of the reasons
I'm advising and hoping

00:59:53.470 --> 00:59:56.440
that the young people will
pick me up on my challenge,

00:59:56.440 --> 00:59:59.820
is so they can bring that code
of ethics into the management

00:59:59.820 --> 01:00:03.605
however, side-- by the side
or back door, it's done.

01:00:03.605 --> 01:00:04.480
Maybe they'll listen.

01:00:04.480 --> 01:00:06.870
I don't know that they
will, but maybe they will.

01:00:06.870 --> 01:00:09.760
And I have to tell you, I
knew all of these gentlemen

01:00:09.760 --> 01:00:11.560
in that meeting prior to this.

01:00:11.560 --> 01:00:14.810
I've had workings with
them for 5 and 1/2 years.

01:00:14.810 --> 01:00:23.660
And I have no doubt that when
the general manager said,

01:00:23.660 --> 01:00:25.280
about halfway
through that caucus,

01:00:25.280 --> 01:00:27.280
am I the only one
that wants to fly?

01:00:27.280 --> 01:00:31.130
I have no doubt in my mind that
if any of the three managers

01:00:31.130 --> 01:00:34.820
had said, I don't
want to fly, that he

01:00:34.820 --> 01:00:38.090
would have folded his briefcase,
and packed it in, and gone

01:00:38.090 --> 01:00:38.930
home.

01:00:38.930 --> 01:00:40.310
No doubt in my mind.

01:00:40.310 --> 01:00:43.460
He was looking for
support from those guys.

01:00:43.460 --> 01:00:46.314
Now that you're on quote,
"the enemy's side,"

01:00:46.314 --> 01:00:47.480
you've taken the other side.

01:00:47.480 --> 01:00:48.800
I don't understand.

01:00:48.800 --> 01:00:50.450
My colleagues would
say the same thing.

01:00:50.450 --> 01:00:51.430
What's happened?

01:00:51.430 --> 01:00:52.850
And the only thing
we've ever been

01:00:52.850 --> 01:00:54.290
able to conclude
amongst ourselves

01:00:54.290 --> 01:00:59.420
was, after attaining this
level, you have to protect it.

01:00:59.420 --> 01:01:02.300
Well, I was promoted to product
engineering manager at Thiokol,

01:01:02.300 --> 01:01:05.500
but I didn't tell you
in my short presentation

01:01:05.500 --> 01:01:08.210
that after 13
months, I gave it up.

01:01:08.210 --> 01:01:11.360
I could no longer
exist in that--

01:01:11.360 --> 01:01:13.220
I don't know the word--

01:01:13.220 --> 01:01:14.240
awful organization.

01:01:14.240 --> 01:01:17.840
I mean, they knew nothing
about how to manage people.

01:01:17.840 --> 01:01:19.860
They could care less
about how it was done,

01:01:19.860 --> 01:01:21.440
and I didn't want
to be a part of it.

01:01:21.440 --> 01:01:23.510
So I went down as a
technical specialist

01:01:23.510 --> 01:01:26.770
to the manager of
applied mechanics

01:01:26.770 --> 01:01:29.950
because I felt I could help the
program the most, the company

01:01:29.950 --> 01:01:32.900
the most, and have the
most satisfaction in my job

01:01:32.900 --> 01:01:35.740
instead of just doing
this mediocre crap

01:01:35.740 --> 01:01:37.660
day after day after day.

01:01:37.660 --> 01:01:39.730
And so I gave up a
managerial position.

01:01:39.730 --> 01:01:41.415
And people around
me were shocked.

01:01:41.415 --> 01:01:44.020
They said, do you
realize what you've done?

01:01:44.020 --> 01:01:48.580
I used to have a ceiling of
$75,000, and when I did that,

01:01:48.580 --> 01:01:50.620
I lost a substantial
amount of ceiling.

01:01:50.620 --> 01:01:51.880
I didn't care.

01:01:51.880 --> 01:01:53.830
I wanted day to
day satisfaction.

01:01:53.830 --> 01:01:56.110
I wanted to do
something in my job,

01:01:56.110 --> 01:01:58.330
but that doesn't make me a hero.

01:01:58.330 --> 01:02:01.210
It just makes me kind of
unique, I guess, in one respect

01:02:01.210 --> 01:02:02.920
because most people
don't think that way.

01:02:02.920 --> 01:02:04.420
After you get a
management position,

01:02:04.420 --> 01:02:06.380
you're going to preserve
it at all costs.

01:02:06.380 --> 01:02:07.660
And I didn't do that.

01:02:07.660 --> 01:02:09.670
And I-- there were
other people that did

01:02:09.670 --> 01:02:11.289
the same types of thing too.

01:02:11.289 --> 01:02:12.580
I'm not unique in that respect.

01:02:12.580 --> 01:02:13.788
I've seen other people do it.

01:02:13.788 --> 01:02:17.080
But by and large, when a person
has attained a certain level,

01:02:17.080 --> 01:02:20.980
they're first and foremost
priority become preservation.

01:02:20.980 --> 01:02:22.690
I've seen it in the 27 years.

01:02:22.690 --> 01:02:23.600
I can't believe it.

01:02:23.600 --> 01:02:25.240
I can't explain why,
but that happens.

01:02:27.697 --> 01:02:29.530
MODERATOR: Well, I think
we're ready to open

01:02:29.530 --> 01:02:31.370
the floor for questions.

01:02:31.370 --> 01:02:33.070
So if you-- those of
you with questions

01:02:33.070 --> 01:02:35.140
will go to the
microphone, then we

01:02:35.140 --> 01:02:39.569
can have your questions
recorded on our video tape.

01:02:47.060 --> 01:02:47.570
Yeah.

01:02:47.570 --> 01:02:49.770
AUDIENCE: You mentioned the
meeting with the Air Force,

01:02:49.770 --> 01:02:51.590
I think it was, at
which your manager gave

01:02:51.590 --> 01:02:52.790
the slanted presentation.

01:02:52.790 --> 01:02:53.840
And you sat in silence.

01:02:53.840 --> 01:02:55.330
I thought you
didn't say anything

01:02:55.330 --> 01:02:57.140
to contradict what he said.

01:02:57.140 --> 01:03:00.740
I would imagine that the Air
Force people there, interpreted

01:03:00.740 --> 01:03:03.110
your presence, at least, as
implicit support for what

01:03:03.110 --> 01:03:04.770
your manager was saying.

01:03:04.770 --> 01:03:05.600
Is that true?

01:03:05.600 --> 01:03:08.480
BOISJOLY: Well, let me tell
you, it really, in all honesty,

01:03:08.480 --> 01:03:11.690
didn't matter what he said
because the part had already

01:03:11.690 --> 01:03:14.510
been designed as
a physical part.

01:03:14.510 --> 01:03:16.760
It had already been
tested as a physical part.

01:03:16.760 --> 01:03:19.250
It worked just fine,
but he was trying

01:03:19.250 --> 01:03:23.180
to puff up a certain section
of an analysis conclusion

01:03:23.180 --> 01:03:25.390
that just wasn't so.

01:03:25.390 --> 01:03:28.190
And he wanted me to do that, OK?

01:03:28.190 --> 01:03:31.990
And so I let him do his thing,
but I would have shot him down.

01:03:31.990 --> 01:03:34.270
But regardless of
what he said, he

01:03:34.270 --> 01:03:37.120
wasn't going to change the
fact that the part was good.

01:03:37.120 --> 01:03:39.375
The design had already
been proven in test,

01:03:39.375 --> 01:03:42.110
and had already been installed,
and was going to be used.

01:03:42.110 --> 01:03:45.070
And so this wasn't a threat.

01:03:45.070 --> 01:03:49.210
This was an issue between him
forcing me to do something

01:03:49.210 --> 01:03:53.980
that I knew was wrong,
and I wouldn't do it,

01:03:53.980 --> 01:03:55.699
and the outcome
was-- absolutely, had

01:03:55.699 --> 01:03:56.990
nothing to do with [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:56.990 --> 01:03:59.031
MODERATOR: Excuse me, are
you talking about the--

01:03:59.031 --> 01:04:01.590
his earlier event that worked
out well in the long run?

01:04:01.590 --> 01:04:03.752
Or are you talking
about the telecom

01:04:03.752 --> 01:04:05.460
the night before the
Challenger disaster?

01:04:05.460 --> 01:04:06.790
AUDIENCE: No, he got it right.

01:04:06.790 --> 01:04:08.040
BOISJOLY: No, yeah, you talk--

01:04:08.040 --> 01:04:09.430
I know what you're--

01:04:09.430 --> 01:04:12.260
you're talking about the
analysis presentation.

01:04:12.260 --> 01:04:14.350
AUDIENCE: I'm just curious
if you're satisfied,

01:04:14.350 --> 01:04:17.710
looking back on that with--

01:04:17.710 --> 01:04:21.740
from a ethical point of view
with your response to that.

01:04:21.740 --> 01:04:23.840
I mean, silence, I
mean, sitting and saying

01:04:23.840 --> 01:04:27.956
nothing that could be considered
deceiving in some way.

01:04:27.956 --> 01:04:28.456
I was--

01:04:28.456 --> 01:04:31.970
BOISJOLY: Well, keep in mind
though, as an individual--

01:04:31.970 --> 01:04:32.770
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I--

01:04:32.770 --> 01:04:33.942
I know.

01:04:33.942 --> 01:04:36.150
BOISJOLY: It doesn't make
a whole lot of sense to me,

01:04:36.150 --> 01:04:39.020
and maybe I'm wrong, but to me,
it doesn't make a lot of sense,

01:04:39.020 --> 01:04:42.530
to speak up and serve yourself
up as a sacrificial lamb,

01:04:42.530 --> 01:04:46.370
if you don't have even a tiny,
tiny chance of making a change

01:04:46.370 --> 01:04:48.850
or effecting a change.

01:04:48.850 --> 01:04:52.340
And if it's not going to
result in any harm downstream

01:04:52.340 --> 01:04:54.125
or make any difference,
what's the point?

01:04:54.125 --> 01:04:55.250
AUDIENCE: OK, so there was-

01:04:55.250 --> 01:04:56.720
BOISJOLY: Now, when it did--

01:04:56.720 --> 01:04:59.180
now, if it had been a
different circumstance,

01:04:59.180 --> 01:05:00.940
and something was on
the verge of failing,

01:05:00.940 --> 01:05:03.470
ho ho, he would not have
given that presentation,

01:05:03.470 --> 01:05:04.286
I assure you.

01:05:04.286 --> 01:05:05.660
I would have told
him I was going

01:05:05.660 --> 01:05:07.220
to get that presentation
like he wanted,

01:05:07.220 --> 01:05:09.178
and then proceeded to
give it the way I wanted.

01:05:16.360 --> 01:05:19.646
AUDIENCE: Good evening,
I'm registered in Canada

01:05:19.646 --> 01:05:20.770
as a professional engineer.

01:05:20.770 --> 01:05:23.350
And I haven't bothered to
transfer my registration down

01:05:23.350 --> 01:05:28.076
here, largely because
I see that the profile

01:05:28.076 --> 01:05:29.950
of professional engineer
in the United States

01:05:29.950 --> 01:05:36.241
just hasn't attained the status
of the Canadian professional

01:05:36.241 --> 01:05:36.740
engineer.

01:05:36.740 --> 01:05:38.560
I was wondering, you--

01:05:38.560 --> 01:05:41.020
I fully agree with
you that registering

01:05:41.020 --> 01:05:43.210
is a darn good thing to do.

01:05:43.210 --> 01:05:46.570
But my question is,
what do you suppose

01:05:46.570 --> 01:05:52.450
we can do as engineers
to raise the profile

01:05:52.450 --> 01:05:57.370
of the professional, the
registered professional?

01:05:57.370 --> 01:05:59.590
BOISJOLY: I think
they're starting

01:05:59.590 --> 01:06:02.560
to do just that in the NSPE.

01:06:02.560 --> 01:06:04.240
And I think you're
right, they kind of

01:06:04.240 --> 01:06:08.230
went along at a mediocre
level for years.

01:06:08.230 --> 01:06:09.820
And I think that
they are starting

01:06:09.820 --> 01:06:12.340
to come to grips with that.

01:06:12.340 --> 01:06:14.290
I think some of the
professional societies

01:06:14.290 --> 01:06:18.100
or the technical
societies are really

01:06:18.100 --> 01:06:21.660
where it needs to start to take
place and feed into the NSPE.

01:06:21.660 --> 01:06:25.630
The technical societies
have got to stop

01:06:25.630 --> 01:06:28.600
serving the industry and
the underpinning resources

01:06:28.600 --> 01:06:31.530
they get from industry, and
start to serve their members.

01:06:31.530 --> 01:06:34.600
And I think you're going to
see a chain reaction spill over

01:06:34.600 --> 01:06:37.290
into the professional
side through the NSPE.

01:06:37.290 --> 01:06:42.630
But I saw the NSPE as a very
passive organization years ago,

01:06:42.630 --> 01:06:44.680
and I see it as a much
more productive and active

01:06:44.680 --> 01:06:46.060
organization today.

01:06:46.060 --> 01:06:51.250
For instance, I met a
colleague in Missouri

01:06:51.250 --> 01:06:55.360
who was on the board at
Missouri when the Hyatt Regency

01:06:55.360 --> 01:06:56.730
failure occurred.

01:06:56.730 --> 01:06:59.200
And they actually
pursued that prosecution

01:06:59.200 --> 01:07:02.800
of that firm and that engineers
for blatant screw ups.

01:07:02.800 --> 01:07:04.150
I mean, it was--

01:07:04.150 --> 01:07:06.910
freshmen would not have
designed those pins the way

01:07:06.910 --> 01:07:08.670
that pin was designed.

01:07:08.670 --> 01:07:10.690
And they've lost their license.

01:07:10.690 --> 01:07:15.010
And I'll tell you, he told
me, he took tremendous flack

01:07:15.010 --> 01:07:16.990
through the profession
for doing what

01:07:16.990 --> 01:07:19.750
he did because he was the
president of the Missouri Board

01:07:19.750 --> 01:07:21.200
at that time.

01:07:21.200 --> 01:07:24.250
And that's an indication
that it's started.

01:07:24.250 --> 01:07:26.020
Also, the other
indication it's started,

01:07:26.020 --> 01:07:28.340
and it's starting really
in the civil arena.

01:07:28.340 --> 01:07:31.060
And it's because the
registration is the strongest

01:07:31.060 --> 01:07:32.620
in the civil arena.

01:07:32.620 --> 01:07:35.500
And in the civil
arena, they've put

01:07:35.500 --> 01:07:39.030
out a product called Quality
in the Constructed Project.

01:07:39.030 --> 01:07:40.190
It's a manual.

01:07:40.190 --> 01:07:42.700
And it is currently
in practice right now

01:07:42.700 --> 01:07:46.480
being used under actual
conditions for review

01:07:46.480 --> 01:07:47.450
and comment.

01:07:47.450 --> 01:07:50.980
And it is due to end, perhaps
at the end of this year,

01:07:50.980 --> 01:07:53.341
and then they will
update that, and expand,

01:07:53.341 --> 01:07:54.340
and put more manuals in.

01:07:54.340 --> 01:07:56.770
But that is a
wonderful step and that

01:07:56.770 --> 01:07:58.840
is feeding in through the NSPE.

01:07:58.840 --> 01:08:01.180
And I think that's
going to go a long way

01:08:01.180 --> 01:08:07.450
to address the past moderation
that was in that group.

01:08:07.450 --> 01:08:09.460
And the civils are
taking the lead position,

01:08:09.460 --> 01:08:12.795
no question about it.

01:08:12.795 --> 01:08:15.260
AUDIENCE: I wanted to
just make a comment,

01:08:15.260 --> 01:08:16.739
and ask you to comment on it.

01:08:16.739 --> 01:08:18.957
But what I gathered
from your-- what

01:08:18.957 --> 01:08:22.408
you said was the conclusion
that one could come to

01:08:22.408 --> 01:08:23.890
is that the goals
of the engineer

01:08:23.890 --> 01:08:26.505
and the goals of the
managers are not the same.

01:08:26.505 --> 01:08:28.979
And that's why there is
this is this constant--

01:08:28.979 --> 01:08:33.300
I mean, the picture that
I drew of your accounts,

01:08:33.300 --> 01:08:36.730
not only here, but also when
you spoke of it five years ago,

01:08:36.730 --> 01:08:40.160
was that there was an
adversarial relationship

01:08:40.160 --> 01:08:43.120
between managers and engineers.

01:08:43.120 --> 01:08:46.510
The reason why that's
interesting is because this

01:08:46.510 --> 01:08:48.955
point was made, actually,
[? by Thorstein ?] [? Veblen ?]

01:08:48.955 --> 01:08:51.400
in [? 1920 ?] in a very
interesting [? monograph ?]

01:08:51.400 --> 01:08:53.682
called The [? Engineer ?]
and [? the ?] [? Price ?]

01:08:53.682 --> 01:08:56.790
[? System, ?] in which he
pointed out that the goals

01:08:56.790 --> 01:09:00.804
of management were
basically bottom line goals,

01:09:00.804 --> 01:09:03.768
whereas the goals of
engineers were efficient.

01:09:03.768 --> 01:09:06.485
And it seems to me
that nothing has

01:09:06.485 --> 01:09:08.708
changed in the last 70 years.

01:09:08.708 --> 01:09:12.824
And I guess what I want to ask
you is [? assume ?] [? that ?]

01:09:12.824 --> 01:09:15.130
[? my ?] [? characters ?]
[INAUDIBLE]

01:09:15.130 --> 01:09:16.118
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

01:09:16.118 --> 01:09:19.481
Does your experiences suggest
that nothing's changed?

01:09:19.481 --> 01:09:21.689
BOISJOLY: No, I don't agree
that nothing has changed.

01:09:21.689 --> 01:09:24.359
It's gotten a lot worse.

01:09:24.359 --> 01:09:26.250
In the last 30 years,
I have witnessed

01:09:26.250 --> 01:09:29.760
an unbelievable
decline and open chasm

01:09:29.760 --> 01:09:33.310
between the technical people
and the managerial side.

01:09:33.310 --> 01:09:35.790
And that has
actually came about,

01:09:35.790 --> 01:09:39.330
as I observed when I entered
the profession in 1960,

01:09:39.330 --> 01:09:43.529
the think tank groups
were implementing

01:09:43.529 --> 01:09:47.657
a program, which we used to call
internally as charm courses.

01:09:47.657 --> 01:09:49.740
Those of us who didn't
attend them would call them

01:09:49.740 --> 01:09:52.060
charm courses, and
what these courses were

01:09:52.060 --> 01:09:54.750
were three to five day
concentrated courses that

01:09:54.750 --> 01:09:57.900
showed management how to extract
the maximum amount of profit

01:09:57.900 --> 01:10:01.320
from an organization,
regardless, and never

01:10:01.320 --> 01:10:03.570
look at the consequences
to that organization,

01:10:03.570 --> 01:10:06.420
or the people in
that organization,

01:10:06.420 --> 01:10:09.060
or the decrease in morale,
and stuff like that.

01:10:09.060 --> 01:10:11.850
And through that period of
time, from about 1960 through

01:10:11.850 --> 01:10:16.770
the middle '70s, it just
became obvious, and just--

01:10:16.770 --> 01:10:20.010
employees were no longer their
resource, precious resource.

01:10:20.010 --> 01:10:22.620
And I referred to them
as a renewable resource,

01:10:22.620 --> 01:10:26.640
and that's exactly what they
became, a source of labor

01:10:26.640 --> 01:10:29.400
pool without any regard
to the humanisation.

01:10:29.400 --> 01:10:31.510
And the chasm between
the technical people

01:10:31.510 --> 01:10:34.650
and the managerial people opened
up much, much widely than it

01:10:34.650 --> 01:10:36.050
was when I was first--

01:10:36.050 --> 01:10:37.860
when I was first
there, there was--

01:10:37.860 --> 01:10:40.886
I thought there was a purpose.

01:10:40.886 --> 01:10:42.510
And the purpose was
towards putting out

01:10:42.510 --> 01:10:46.070
a good product, a quality
product, a safe product,

01:10:46.070 --> 01:10:49.410
on time, at a competitive
price, on a teamwork basis.

01:10:49.410 --> 01:10:53.419
We used to always check
one another's work.

01:10:53.419 --> 01:10:54.960
There is no checking
done, and hasn't

01:10:54.960 --> 01:10:58.856
been done since approximately
1970, or even the late '60s.

01:10:58.856 --> 01:11:00.840
Very-- I don't
know of any company

01:11:00.840 --> 01:11:03.970
that has the technical people
check one another's work.

01:11:03.970 --> 01:11:05.862
And so the interest
is very obvious.

01:11:05.862 --> 01:11:08.070
The obvious interest is to
get something out the door

01:11:08.070 --> 01:11:09.430
to get money back in.

01:11:09.430 --> 01:11:10.500
Keep the pipeline filled.

01:11:10.500 --> 01:11:12.542
Everybody at the top
smiles and is happy.

01:11:12.542 --> 01:11:13.500
It's as simple as that.

01:11:13.500 --> 01:11:15.600
I mean, that's
oversimplification,

01:11:15.600 --> 01:11:17.077
but that's the bottom line.

01:11:17.077 --> 01:11:18.660
MODERATOR: I have a
follow on question

01:11:18.660 --> 01:11:20.125
I'd like to ask about that.

01:11:20.125 --> 01:11:21.750
Because one of the
things that we see--

01:11:21.750 --> 01:11:24.420
the 273 students have been
interviewing companies

01:11:24.420 --> 01:11:25.810
over the last few years.

01:11:25.810 --> 01:11:27.900
And a lot of them have
made within the last,

01:11:27.900 --> 01:11:30.630
oh, I should say,
mm, seven years,

01:11:30.630 --> 01:11:32.950
they've made major changes.

01:11:32.950 --> 01:11:35.360
And they've put in ombudsmen.

01:11:35.360 --> 01:11:39.780
They've put in mechanisms
to be more responsive.

01:11:39.780 --> 01:11:43.410
And even if we say that some
of that is window dressing,

01:11:43.410 --> 01:11:47.450
I'd be interested to find out
how people see things going

01:11:47.450 --> 01:11:49.200
because I've been
impressed that, at least

01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:52.940
at some corporations, things
have gotten much better.

01:11:52.940 --> 01:11:54.510
BOISJOLY: Yeah, I
subscribe to that.

01:11:54.510 --> 01:11:58.200
I know of several corporations
with sure knowledge

01:11:58.200 --> 01:11:59.970
that it is working.

01:11:59.970 --> 01:12:02.320
It has been implemented
and is great.

01:12:02.320 --> 01:12:04.060
But unfortunately,
at most of them,

01:12:04.060 --> 01:12:05.310
they're in a learning process.

01:12:05.310 --> 01:12:07.470
In all fairness,
first of all, you

01:12:07.470 --> 01:12:09.840
have to give them credit
for first of all recognizing

01:12:09.840 --> 01:12:11.709
that they have a problem.

01:12:11.709 --> 01:12:13.500
Can't correct anything
unless you recognize

01:12:13.500 --> 01:12:15.570
you have a problem,
and almost all of them

01:12:15.570 --> 01:12:17.100
have recognized that.

01:12:17.100 --> 01:12:18.690
Now, you have to
segregate those that

01:12:18.690 --> 01:12:20.640
recognize they have a
problem and truly want

01:12:20.640 --> 01:12:23.430
to fix it, and do something
about it versus those that

01:12:23.430 --> 01:12:26.280
want to just superficially fix
it, and do something about it.

01:12:26.280 --> 01:12:28.500
And I think we're
in that muddled area

01:12:28.500 --> 01:12:31.200
right now, where some
are truly fixing,

01:12:31.200 --> 01:12:33.000
and some aren't truly fixing.

01:12:33.000 --> 01:12:36.090
Case in point, as a result of
the Presidential Commission

01:12:36.090 --> 01:12:39.780
investigation, NASA was to
set up a reporting system

01:12:39.780 --> 01:12:41.580
that people could
report problems

01:12:41.580 --> 01:12:44.850
through the organization,
to a separate organization,

01:12:44.850 --> 01:12:49.200
and remain totally autonomous,
and not be identified.

01:12:49.200 --> 01:12:52.270
That system was
immediately destroyed

01:12:52.270 --> 01:12:58.440
as several very diligent
people, used that system,

01:12:58.440 --> 01:13:00.330
and found out that
their names were

01:13:00.330 --> 01:13:02.250
blurted out all over the place.

01:13:02.250 --> 01:13:05.550
It killed the effectiveness
of that system right out

01:13:05.550 --> 01:13:09.370
of the chute, and that
system is just a front now.

01:13:09.370 --> 01:13:11.790
And so you have
those, and you have

01:13:11.790 --> 01:13:13.470
those that are
working moderately,

01:13:13.470 --> 01:13:15.178
and you have those
that are working well.

01:13:15.178 --> 01:13:18.330
And I have to say, and that's
why I've made it in my comment

01:13:18.330 --> 01:13:20.070
from my observation,
that I believe

01:13:20.070 --> 01:13:21.995
that most all the
companies recognize

01:13:21.995 --> 01:13:24.120
they're in serious competitive
trouble in our world

01:13:24.120 --> 01:13:27.277
marketplace, and productivity
is a rock-bottom low,

01:13:27.277 --> 01:13:29.110
and that they have to
do something about it.

01:13:29.110 --> 01:13:32.400
And they are putting
these in place.

01:13:32.400 --> 01:13:36.220
And unfortunately, I don't
have any more insight than that

01:13:36.220 --> 01:13:38.132
by observation.

01:13:38.132 --> 01:13:39.090
Freada, you might have.

01:13:41.710 --> 01:13:44.420
KLEIN: Well, I tend
to agree with you.

01:13:44.420 --> 01:13:49.990
The mechanisms that companies
are putting in place,

01:13:49.990 --> 01:13:52.450
in most cases, it
is window dressing.

01:13:52.450 --> 01:13:54.670
It is too little, too late.

01:13:54.670 --> 01:13:58.060
And the fundamental concept
of empowering employees

01:13:58.060 --> 01:14:01.090
to speak up and to
use that information

01:14:01.090 --> 01:14:07.000
to change decisions as well
as decision-making structures,

01:14:07.000 --> 01:14:09.490
when it gets to that
critical point, that's

01:14:09.490 --> 01:14:12.080
when companies back down.

01:14:12.080 --> 01:14:15.610
So that certain kinds of
suggestions, certain kinds

01:14:15.610 --> 01:14:19.210
of changes in
production processes,

01:14:19.210 --> 01:14:22.120
in how business is
done, will be accepted

01:14:22.120 --> 01:14:23.690
through those channels.

01:14:23.690 --> 01:14:25.690
Things that question,
fundamentally,

01:14:25.690 --> 01:14:28.490
the way the organization
is structured,

01:14:28.490 --> 01:14:33.530
which may be causing some of the
problems, are not admissible.

01:14:33.530 --> 01:14:38.170
I think just to be fair, there
is a larger context going on

01:14:38.170 --> 01:14:38.800
here.

01:14:38.800 --> 01:14:43.810
I mean, CEOs talk about
the pressure put on them

01:14:43.810 --> 01:14:47.470
by their boards of directors,
by the shareholders

01:14:47.470 --> 01:14:50.920
because of the power that the
Wall Street analysts wield,

01:14:50.920 --> 01:14:55.030
and that they are held
only to short-term--

01:14:55.030 --> 01:14:59.300
short-term earnings, which
leads to short-term thinking.

01:14:59.300 --> 01:15:02.390
So I think we've got a much
more complicated problem here.

01:15:06.790 --> 01:15:08.680
MODERATOR: Yes.

01:15:08.680 --> 01:15:11.150
AUDIENCE: I have a
question for Mr. Boisjoly

01:15:11.150 --> 01:15:13.360
and a question for the panel.

01:15:13.360 --> 01:15:16.180
Question for Mr. Boisjoly
is about your journal.

01:15:16.180 --> 01:15:20.110
Could you describe when
you decided to start

01:15:20.110 --> 01:15:22.423
writing a journal and why?

01:15:22.423 --> 01:15:26.080
BOISJOLY: Yeah, I
never kept a journal.

01:15:26.080 --> 01:15:28.030
I virtually never
documented anything,

01:15:28.030 --> 01:15:31.450
except in the memo format
that I explained to you.

01:15:31.450 --> 01:15:34.600
And what was happening
is that the problems were

01:15:34.600 --> 01:15:39.900
getting so bad, that-- and
I was getting so afraid.

01:15:39.900 --> 01:15:42.730
See, people think it was just
a low temperature problem.

01:15:42.730 --> 01:15:48.670
I was afraid, after the summer
of 1985, to launch at all,

01:15:48.670 --> 01:15:50.670
and that's why I wrote my memo.

01:15:50.670 --> 01:15:52.730
And I started to
document everything

01:15:52.730 --> 01:15:55.360
and, actually, filled
four of these spiral

01:15:55.360 --> 01:15:57.930
wound notebooks with
my documentation.

01:15:57.930 --> 01:16:02.420
I had a daily log because of
the fear of what was happening,

01:16:02.420 --> 01:16:04.480
and that there
would be no record,

01:16:04.480 --> 01:16:06.220
other than a verbal
record, to go back

01:16:06.220 --> 01:16:07.270
to if something happened.

01:16:07.270 --> 01:16:10.305
And that's really why I did it.

01:16:10.305 --> 01:16:11.680
AUDIENCE: And the
second question

01:16:11.680 --> 01:16:15.910
is, given that that
framework that you described

01:16:15.910 --> 01:16:19.060
was destroyed, do
any of the panelists

01:16:19.060 --> 01:16:24.700
have suggestions on how you
would create such a framework,

01:16:24.700 --> 01:16:27.880
whether it was through
the professional engineers

01:16:27.880 --> 01:16:30.130
or some other organization?

01:16:30.130 --> 01:16:32.600
Given that it doesn't
work, apparently, now.

01:16:32.600 --> 01:16:34.510
MARKS: You mean about
the particular status

01:16:34.510 --> 01:16:36.070
of the engineer and--

01:16:36.070 --> 01:16:39.760
AUDIENCE: Yeah, how the feedback
mechanism could be done,

01:16:39.760 --> 01:16:41.980
could be made to work.

01:16:41.980 --> 01:16:44.160
A whistle-blower
mechanism, however you

01:16:44.160 --> 01:16:47.280
want to describe it, an
independent secure channel.

01:16:51.090 --> 01:16:53.859
MODERATOR: Would you
like to start, Dave?

01:16:53.859 --> 01:16:55.650
MARKS: When did you
stop beating your wife?

01:16:58.680 --> 01:17:04.350
I guess, I don't totally
agree with Mr. Boisjoly.

01:17:04.350 --> 01:17:09.060
I think that there are times
when engineering management

01:17:09.060 --> 01:17:12.060
wants things that
are probably good,

01:17:12.060 --> 01:17:15.090
and engineers don't
want to give it to them.

01:17:15.090 --> 01:17:16.530
I mean, I see
numerous instances,

01:17:16.530 --> 01:17:19.320
where engineers want
the technical challenge

01:17:19.320 --> 01:17:22.080
in a new job of
redesigning something,

01:17:22.080 --> 01:17:24.000
when, in fact, they have
something that works,

01:17:24.000 --> 01:17:26.400
and that could
immediately be adapted.

01:17:26.400 --> 01:17:29.480
But they want the
technical challenge.

01:17:29.480 --> 01:17:31.230
So these terrible
engineering managers

01:17:31.230 --> 01:17:32.910
are trying to get
more productivity,

01:17:32.910 --> 01:17:35.640
and sometimes, they have
good reason to ask for it.

01:17:35.640 --> 01:17:38.040
And the engineering
profession really wants

01:17:38.040 --> 01:17:39.840
their sense of a
rewarding career, which

01:17:39.840 --> 01:17:42.210
is to do reanalysis, to
start all over again,

01:17:42.210 --> 01:17:43.980
and very often to
come up with things

01:17:43.980 --> 01:17:46.540
that need to be tested again.

01:17:46.540 --> 01:17:48.751
And they reinvent the wheel.

01:17:48.751 --> 01:17:50.250
But then there's
another part of it,

01:17:50.250 --> 01:17:52.980
which is the safety human
values thing, where,

01:17:52.980 --> 01:17:58.920
in fact, engineering management
short-term philosophy is just

01:17:58.920 --> 01:18:02.220
acting diametrically
opposed to public safety

01:18:02.220 --> 01:18:03.270
and to employee rights.

01:18:03.270 --> 01:18:07.140
So I-- this movement
of an engineer

01:18:07.140 --> 01:18:12.300
from the technical to a
management line is never easy.

01:18:12.300 --> 01:18:15.600
The people who tend to follow
it are often the more--

01:18:15.600 --> 01:18:19.110
the more-- the less
technically happy, and the more

01:18:19.110 --> 01:18:20.190
management oriented.

01:18:20.190 --> 01:18:21.867
They see more
money to be gained,

01:18:21.867 --> 01:18:22.950
and these sorts of things.

01:18:22.950 --> 01:18:25.770
And they're under a lot of
pressures, not all of which

01:18:25.770 --> 01:18:28.860
are bad pressures to begin with.

01:18:28.860 --> 01:18:32.460
But coming back to your question
about what happens to the role

01:18:32.460 --> 01:18:34.080
and to the status
of technologists.

01:18:34.080 --> 01:18:36.534
I mean, it's no
surprise that the number

01:18:36.534 --> 01:18:37.950
of people in the
United States who

01:18:37.950 --> 01:18:41.010
want to go into technical
careers is going down.

01:18:41.010 --> 01:18:45.930
We're finding the biggest
recruiter of our undergraduates

01:18:45.930 --> 01:18:47.730
to be the big eight
accounting firms now,

01:18:47.730 --> 01:18:49.800
who want the strong
analytic abilities,

01:18:49.800 --> 01:18:52.320
and the ability to move
these people into finance.

01:18:52.320 --> 01:18:53.500
That's where the action is.

01:18:53.500 --> 01:18:54.999
That's where the
immediate money is.

01:18:54.999 --> 01:18:58.260
That's where there is less--

01:18:58.260 --> 01:19:02.570
one has to pay less of
a price for education

01:19:02.570 --> 01:19:04.750
and get greater rewards.

01:19:04.750 --> 01:19:07.260
What are we doing
to counter this?

01:19:07.260 --> 01:19:10.650
I think, we haven't
given much recognition

01:19:10.650 --> 01:19:12.280
to the importance of technology.

01:19:12.280 --> 01:19:13.620
I don't see how that's
going to turn around.

01:19:13.620 --> 01:19:15.060
It starts in the high schools.

01:19:15.060 --> 01:19:19.950
Look at the level of science
and mathematical role models

01:19:19.950 --> 01:19:21.600
and teachers in high school.

01:19:21.600 --> 01:19:24.130
It is just simply not there.

01:19:24.130 --> 01:19:26.010
So to a certain
extent, engineers

01:19:26.010 --> 01:19:30.360
are defeating themselves because
they're not even bringing

01:19:30.360 --> 01:19:31.510
people into the pipeline.

01:19:31.510 --> 01:19:35.221
They're not making the
pipeline look attractive,

01:19:35.221 --> 01:19:36.720
and this country
is paying for that.

01:19:39.872 --> 01:19:41.830
MODERATOR: Jim, did you
want to speak to that--

01:19:41.830 --> 01:19:43.180
MELCHER: Well, I
was going to pick up

01:19:43.180 --> 01:19:45.400
on something that was
inherent to the two remarks.

01:19:45.400 --> 01:19:48.310
And that is this feeling
that some of us have,

01:19:48.310 --> 01:19:52.090
that the engineering culture
would be well transplanted

01:19:52.090 --> 01:19:53.930
into the managerial
scheme of things.

01:19:53.930 --> 01:19:56.050
That if engineers
turned into managers,

01:19:56.050 --> 01:19:59.480
it might be good for management.

01:19:59.480 --> 01:20:01.270
We put things in a
rather negative light.

01:20:01.270 --> 01:20:02.520
Do you disagree with that?

01:20:02.520 --> 01:20:04.930
BOISJOLY: Oh, no.

01:20:04.930 --> 01:20:08.110
No, I think some of
the better managers

01:20:08.110 --> 01:20:11.290
had come from the
engineering ranks.

01:20:11.290 --> 01:20:14.674
But unfortunately, the pressures
of the business side of it

01:20:14.674 --> 01:20:16.090
made them change
their allegiance.

01:20:16.090 --> 01:20:16.864
But as far as--

01:20:16.864 --> 01:20:19.030
one of the better-- best
managers at Morton-Thiokol,

01:20:19.030 --> 01:20:21.430
for instance, was Al McDonald.

01:20:21.430 --> 01:20:24.520
Al McDonald is the guy that
actually pulled the redesign

01:20:24.520 --> 01:20:26.260
back together because
the other company

01:20:26.260 --> 01:20:27.670
managers couldn't do it.

01:20:27.670 --> 01:20:30.520
Right in the middle of the
redesign to return to flight,

01:20:30.520 --> 01:20:31.810
they fell flat in their face.

01:20:31.810 --> 01:20:34.960
And they had to actually call
on him to straighten it out,

01:20:34.960 --> 01:20:38.860
and his engineering expertise
is what allowed him to do that.

01:20:38.860 --> 01:20:41.180
And his-- the way he thought
allowed him to do that.

01:20:41.180 --> 01:20:43.390
So if you can combine
the two, I think

01:20:43.390 --> 01:20:47.800
you get an excellent result
for a person who really cares.

01:20:47.800 --> 01:20:49.420
Person who has
product, and company,

01:20:49.420 --> 01:20:55.150
and customer at the foremost
position and goal of his task.

01:20:55.150 --> 01:20:57.159
I think you get a
terrific marriage,

01:20:57.159 --> 01:20:58.450
when you do that type of thing.

01:21:01.927 --> 01:21:04.426
MODERATOR: Oh, and did you want
to respond to that question?

01:21:04.426 --> 01:21:04.930
OK.

01:21:04.930 --> 01:21:06.104
Yes.

01:21:06.104 --> 01:21:08.020
AUDIENCE: Oh, Mr. Boisjoly,
you mentioned that

01:21:08.020 --> 01:21:10.570
in a number of the ethical
decisions that you had to make,

01:21:10.570 --> 01:21:13.620
the consequences of
them were severe.

01:21:13.620 --> 01:21:16.570
Somebody's life was at risk.

01:21:16.570 --> 01:21:20.650
And what my question is, is
how much do the consequences

01:21:20.650 --> 01:21:26.380
play in your decision of whether
to go to do the ethical thing?

01:21:26.380 --> 01:21:30.320
For a number of us, we'll
be in engineering field--

01:21:30.320 --> 01:21:33.130
we'll be in engineering
fields that won't necessarily

01:21:33.130 --> 01:21:36.250
have those kinds
of severe risks,

01:21:36.250 --> 01:21:39.550
like perhaps you're
degrading an error

01:21:39.550 --> 01:21:42.660
rate on an IBM [INAUDIBLE]
or something similar.

01:21:42.660 --> 01:21:45.130
BOISJOLY: Well, yeah,
I mentioned some

01:21:45.130 --> 01:21:46.390
of the more visible things.

01:21:46.390 --> 01:21:48.070
But there were a
whole host of things

01:21:48.070 --> 01:21:52.310
that I didn't mention that were
not only not life threatening,

01:21:52.310 --> 01:21:54.730
they were not even product
threatening or anything

01:21:54.730 --> 01:21:56.290
threatening period.

01:21:56.290 --> 01:21:59.210
But they were just
the wrong thing to do.

01:21:59.210 --> 01:22:02.620
And I don't mean that from
a judgmental standpoint.

01:22:02.620 --> 01:22:04.950
For instance, there
was a part that was

01:22:04.950 --> 01:22:08.410
stalled backwards on a missile.

01:22:08.410 --> 01:22:11.835
It was a band, and it
was formed as a hoop.

01:22:11.835 --> 01:22:14.500
And it was a very critical part.

01:22:14.500 --> 01:22:15.510
It cost about $25.

01:22:18.250 --> 01:22:20.920
They wanted to take that,
and reform it, and reverse

01:22:20.920 --> 01:22:22.780
the hoop, and put it
back on the missile.

01:22:22.780 --> 01:22:25.910
Well, it just so happens, I
was on the site at the time,

01:22:25.910 --> 01:22:27.970
and I didn't want
them to do that.

01:22:27.970 --> 01:22:30.272
I didn't even want to
spend the time running

01:22:30.272 --> 01:22:32.230
an analysis showing
whether it was good or not.

01:22:32.230 --> 01:22:34.750
It wasn't worth the hassle.

01:22:34.750 --> 01:22:41.000
$25 is not worth ruining
$800,000 worth of equipment.

01:22:41.000 --> 01:22:44.060
And they wanted to put it back
in stores and mark it scrap.

01:22:44.060 --> 01:22:48.107
And I said, well, I don't--
to myself, I don't trust you.

01:22:48.107 --> 01:22:49.690
Would you please let
me have the part?

01:22:49.690 --> 01:22:51.340
I took the part folded
it in half, put it

01:22:51.340 --> 01:22:52.548
on the ground, stepped on it.

01:22:52.548 --> 01:22:55.990
It was totally
non-usable at that point.

01:22:55.990 --> 01:22:59.410
They went absolutely wild
when I did that and said,

01:22:59.410 --> 01:23:01.005
they were going to
file a grievance.

01:23:01.005 --> 01:23:02.380
And they were
going to take me up

01:23:02.380 --> 01:23:04.520
to all kinds of
management on this.

01:23:04.520 --> 01:23:06.550
I said, fine, let's go.

01:23:06.550 --> 01:23:08.300
Nothing ever came of that.

01:23:08.300 --> 01:23:10.270
And so that was totally
non-threatening,

01:23:10.270 --> 01:23:12.542
totally not anything,
and I've had many

01:23:12.542 --> 01:23:13.750
of those types of things too.

01:23:13.750 --> 01:23:17.200
It just-- it doesn't make sense
to risk multi-million dollar

01:23:17.200 --> 01:23:19.690
systems for $25 and $30 pieces.

01:23:19.690 --> 01:23:21.070
It's just not
worth fussing with.

01:23:21.070 --> 01:23:22.720
And if you know there's
something wrong,

01:23:22.720 --> 01:23:24.303
something's been
done wrong, just take

01:23:24.303 --> 01:23:26.680
care of it on an inside issue.

01:23:26.680 --> 01:23:28.370
That's all.

01:23:28.370 --> 01:23:32.607
So I'm just the way
I am on everything.

01:23:32.607 --> 01:23:34.940
AUDIENCE: First of all, I
apologize for being very late.

01:23:34.940 --> 01:23:36.315
This wasn't
advertised, and I had

01:23:36.315 --> 01:23:38.530
no idea this wonderful
meeting was going on.

01:23:38.530 --> 01:23:40.370
But I'm glad I'm here anyway.

01:23:40.370 --> 01:23:43.190
And I'd like to ask a
couple of questions.

01:23:43.190 --> 01:23:45.910
The first one relates
to the statement

01:23:45.910 --> 01:23:49.360
made that there's no point
in sacrificing yourself

01:23:49.360 --> 01:23:51.080
if it can't make a difference.

01:23:51.080 --> 01:23:53.560
And there have been a number
of statements we've heard,

01:23:53.560 --> 01:23:56.260
which talk in
terms of trade-off.

01:23:56.260 --> 01:24:00.220
Is it worth doing this in terms
of the consequences we'll get?

01:24:00.220 --> 01:24:02.740
And one of my major problems
with the engineering

01:24:02.740 --> 01:24:04.720
field as a whole
is the way in which

01:24:04.720 --> 01:24:08.830
it tends to operate in
a consequentialist vein.

01:24:08.830 --> 01:24:11.710
Philosophers today,
almost all of them,

01:24:11.710 --> 01:24:14.950
would say the consequentialist
ways of thinking

01:24:14.950 --> 01:24:16.590
are highly unsatisfactory.

01:24:16.590 --> 01:24:18.790
And the community
of philosophers

01:24:18.790 --> 01:24:22.150
has increasingly been
emphasizing issues of rights.

01:24:22.150 --> 01:24:26.320
What is the right thing to
do, whatever the consequences?

01:24:26.320 --> 01:24:28.000
If something is
truth, should I not

01:24:28.000 --> 01:24:32.380
say that truth whatever will
happen to me as a result?

01:24:32.380 --> 01:24:35.470
Should I simply shirk
that responsibility

01:24:35.470 --> 01:24:37.720
because I don't think it
will make a difference?

01:24:37.720 --> 01:24:41.870
Should one not go for justice,
whatever the consequences?

01:24:41.870 --> 01:24:43.550
And I think that's
a fundamental point.

01:24:43.550 --> 01:24:47.200
And I think it comes not just
into everyday decisions which

01:24:47.200 --> 01:24:48.640
engineers make.

01:24:48.640 --> 01:24:51.700
But I think it also comes into
the methods which engineers

01:24:51.700 --> 01:24:54.070
use because
quantitative techniques,

01:24:54.070 --> 01:24:57.260
by their definition,
are calculative,

01:24:57.260 --> 01:25:00.220
and look at trade-offs,
and look at consequences,

01:25:00.220 --> 01:25:02.590
and look at the best for
whatever the problem happens

01:25:02.590 --> 01:25:03.670
to be.

01:25:03.670 --> 01:25:06.400
Whereas perhaps a more
satisfactory approach

01:25:06.400 --> 01:25:08.740
would be to say not
what is the best

01:25:08.740 --> 01:25:12.640
under these certain constraints,
but what questions should we

01:25:12.640 --> 01:25:13.720
be asking?

01:25:13.720 --> 01:25:15.280
What is the best?

01:25:15.280 --> 01:25:18.790
What is the right way
to do our analysis?

01:25:18.790 --> 01:25:20.980
So I'd like you to
address this issue

01:25:20.980 --> 01:25:23.680
of a consequentialist
way of thinking

01:25:23.680 --> 01:25:25.750
and a right way of thinking.

01:25:25.750 --> 01:25:29.770
BOISJOLY: Well, first of
all, if you acted that way

01:25:29.770 --> 01:25:32.470
in a purist sense,
there would be knowing--

01:25:32.470 --> 01:25:36.400
no one in the industry cranking
out anything for anybody.

01:25:36.400 --> 01:25:39.340
Because the system is
so broken with regard

01:25:39.340 --> 01:25:41.420
to those types of
things, in general,

01:25:41.420 --> 01:25:44.510
that if everybody come
forth with every issue,

01:25:44.510 --> 01:25:47.540
regardless of the
consequence, there'd

01:25:47.540 --> 01:25:51.230
be nobody working because
they'd all be out of jobs.

01:25:51.230 --> 01:25:54.430
I know that sounds very drastic,
but that's the way it is.

01:25:54.430 --> 01:25:59.540
If everybody came through,
not to be around another day

01:25:59.540 --> 01:26:02.987
to fight for the overall thing,
you'd have nobody working.

01:26:02.987 --> 01:26:04.070
AUDIENCE: I beg to differ.

01:26:04.070 --> 01:26:09.050
I think that the scenario is
one between people behaving

01:26:09.050 --> 01:26:10.730
as machines, in
which they are using

01:26:10.730 --> 01:26:12.700
standard techniques,
standard procedure,

01:26:12.700 --> 01:26:16.040
slotted into some big
bureaucratic mechanism,

01:26:16.040 --> 01:26:18.170
and people who are
taking responsibility

01:26:18.170 --> 01:26:19.550
for the work which
they're doing.

01:26:19.550 --> 01:26:21.050
BOISJOLY: Have you
worked in industry?

01:26:21.050 --> 01:26:22.100
AUDIENCE: I certainly
have and I--

01:26:22.100 --> 01:26:23.300
BOISJOLY: In the
aerospace industry?

01:26:23.300 --> 01:26:24.800
AUDIENCE: Not in
aerospace industry,

01:26:24.800 --> 01:26:28.990
but I've been a whistle-blower
in a very large scale case.

01:26:28.990 --> 01:26:34.760
And so I have some experience
of these questions.

01:26:34.760 --> 01:26:38.270
And I was quite satisfied
with my decision

01:26:38.270 --> 01:26:42.140
to protest what I regarded as
an unethical form of computer

01:26:42.140 --> 01:26:44.180
modeling, even though
it seemed to me that I

01:26:44.180 --> 01:26:47.690
had no chance of doing
anything about it at that time.

01:26:47.690 --> 01:26:50.300
And I was not satisfied
with doing my job,

01:26:50.300 --> 01:26:52.550
and carrying on doing
computations, when

01:26:52.550 --> 01:26:56.630
I found that that
was what I regarded

01:26:56.630 --> 01:26:58.400
as an unethical,
immoral situation.

01:26:58.400 --> 01:27:00.530
And I tried to influence
the people in the place

01:27:00.530 --> 01:27:04.100
where I was working to try
and take this larger view.

01:27:04.100 --> 01:27:07.570
I think one can, of
course, both do work,

01:27:07.570 --> 01:27:09.570
and one can think about
it at the same time.

01:27:09.570 --> 01:27:13.820
I think where engineering falls
down is the lack of thinking.

01:27:13.820 --> 01:27:17.120
Furthermore, I think that
what we need educationally

01:27:17.120 --> 01:27:19.264
is more training
in how to think.

01:27:19.264 --> 01:27:21.305
I, frankly, despair as I
go through the corridors

01:27:21.305 --> 01:27:23.870
here and see the advertisements
for this new ethics

01:27:23.870 --> 01:27:29.030
course here, which ask
questions about what I regard

01:27:29.030 --> 01:27:30.920
as somewhat obvious questions.

01:27:30.920 --> 01:27:32.760
Should one make a
product that is unsafe?

01:27:32.760 --> 01:27:33.980
Well, the answer to me is no.

01:27:33.980 --> 01:27:36.800
I mean, that's as
far as it goes.

01:27:36.800 --> 01:27:40.460
And perhaps there are some
things to discuss around that.

01:27:40.460 --> 01:27:42.830
But that doesn't seem to be
the most important point.

01:27:42.830 --> 01:27:45.110
The most important
point seems to be

01:27:45.110 --> 01:27:47.700
what product should
we be making?

01:27:47.700 --> 01:27:50.330
What methods should we be using?

01:27:50.330 --> 01:27:52.040
What do our techniques imply?

01:27:52.040 --> 01:27:53.180
What should they imply?

01:27:53.180 --> 01:27:55.700
How should we do that
which is good and just?

01:27:55.700 --> 01:27:58.640
And our basic
failure is a failure

01:27:58.640 --> 01:28:01.187
to confront issues of justice.

01:28:01.187 --> 01:28:03.020
And I think the profession
would be improved

01:28:03.020 --> 01:28:04.526
if we started on that basis.

01:28:04.526 --> 01:28:05.900
And I think good
work could still

01:28:05.900 --> 01:28:07.626
be done within that framework.

01:28:07.626 --> 01:28:09.980
BOISJOLY: Oh, I
agree, but aren't

01:28:09.980 --> 01:28:13.280
you presupposing that the
person at the operational level,

01:28:13.280 --> 01:28:16.970
low level, has the authority and
power to do what you just said?

01:28:16.970 --> 01:28:18.450
It doesn't exist.

01:28:18.450 --> 01:28:21.140
You're not given the freedoms
that you just mentioned.

01:28:21.140 --> 01:28:24.410
In fact, the redesign, case in
point, on the shuttle program

01:28:24.410 --> 01:28:26.630
was not designed by
the working engineer.

01:28:26.630 --> 01:28:29.480
It was designed and
redesigned by the managers.

01:28:29.480 --> 01:28:32.494
And it was up to the individual
engineers to make it work.

01:28:32.494 --> 01:28:34.160
And so they didn't
even have the freedom

01:28:34.160 --> 01:28:35.330
to come up with the ideas.

01:28:35.330 --> 01:28:37.040
We had plenty of ideas.

01:28:37.040 --> 01:28:40.550
We had 27 concepts that
were summarily dismissed.

01:28:40.550 --> 01:28:43.790
So when you work in the
framework of not even being

01:28:43.790 --> 01:28:45.572
allowed to do
something like that,

01:28:45.572 --> 01:28:47.280
you don't have a prayer
out of the chute.

01:28:47.280 --> 01:28:49.790
AUDIENCE: Ah, but if you start
from the position of insisting

01:28:49.790 --> 01:28:51.560
on asking these
questions, you can empower

01:28:51.560 --> 01:28:54.440
yourself to do these things.

01:28:54.440 --> 01:28:57.140
If as a group, we
all forced ourselves

01:28:57.140 --> 01:28:59.570
to do what we thought
was ethically correct,

01:28:59.570 --> 01:29:01.220
and go for those
larger issues, we

01:29:01.220 --> 01:29:03.050
would be able to
have some influence.

01:29:03.050 --> 01:29:05.870
One of the things which made me
despair the most where I worked

01:29:05.870 --> 01:29:09.140
was how the majority of
the other technical people

01:29:09.140 --> 01:29:11.060
just went and did
what they were told.

01:29:11.060 --> 01:29:13.430
And I often had
subversive little tours

01:29:13.430 --> 01:29:15.770
around the floor I worked
on and said to people, look,

01:29:15.770 --> 01:29:19.580
if only all of us
discussed these issues

01:29:19.580 --> 01:29:21.260
and made them known
to management,

01:29:21.260 --> 01:29:22.850
then we would have an effect.

01:29:22.850 --> 01:29:24.860
We shouldn't allow
ourselves to be machines.

01:29:24.860 --> 01:29:28.400
We should be part of the
major decision apparatus.

01:29:28.400 --> 01:29:31.670
BOISJOLY: OK, I made that point
in my presentation, that one

01:29:31.670 --> 01:29:34.130
of the first things you should
do is go get [? colleagal ?]

01:29:34.130 --> 01:29:35.210
support--

01:29:35.210 --> 01:29:38.250
first, verbally,
then in writing.

01:29:38.250 --> 01:29:39.901
And so I guess I'm agreeing.

01:29:39.901 --> 01:29:42.150
MODERATOR: I think we have
another question over here.

01:29:42.150 --> 01:29:44.700
AUDIENCE: Well, I don't have a
question about the most recent

01:29:44.700 --> 01:29:48.199
exchange from [? Bellsley ?]
[? rise ?] [? and speak ?]

01:29:48.199 --> 01:29:52.191
as a [INAUDIBLE] [? for that ?]
[? heated exchange ?]

01:29:52.191 --> 01:29:55.434
[? and no ?] [? elation ?]
for our society or what is

01:29:55.434 --> 01:29:58.179
happening to the
use of technology.

01:29:58.179 --> 01:30:02.670
[? Close their eyes ?]
[INAUDIBLE] Nevertheless,

01:30:02.670 --> 01:30:06.163
the compelling evidence
is that [? in the years ?]

01:30:06.163 --> 01:30:10.654
and technology have been helping
people for their humane needs,

01:30:10.654 --> 01:30:14.147
not for their problems
short-term or long-term

01:30:14.147 --> 01:30:15.145
for their humane needs.

01:30:15.145 --> 01:30:19.636
Now, let me provide some
of the evidence that

01:30:19.636 --> 01:30:22.131
lead to this conclusion.

01:30:22.131 --> 01:30:25.125
More people go to school
today then ever before.

01:30:25.125 --> 01:30:28.617
And they do that only in
technologically advanced

01:30:28.617 --> 01:30:29.117
society.

01:30:29.117 --> 01:30:32.111
More people live longer
today then ever before.

01:30:32.111 --> 01:30:35.604
And they do so only with
technologically advanced

01:30:35.604 --> 01:30:36.602
society.

01:30:36.602 --> 01:30:38.847
[INAUDIBLE] [? accidents ?]
[INAUDIBLE] all

01:30:38.847 --> 01:30:40.594
the technological
activity that you

01:30:40.594 --> 01:30:45.084
have, over the past 80 or 90
years, as we are going down

01:30:45.084 --> 01:30:45.584
[INAUDIBLE].

01:30:45.584 --> 01:30:48.079
And if all the products
that those managers,

01:30:48.079 --> 01:30:52.570
you referred to, were produced
without caring about people

01:30:52.570 --> 01:30:56.562
or the dangers as the
whole system you need to be

01:30:56.562 --> 01:31:02.051
[INAUDIBLE] by some of the
people that think it's falling

01:31:02.051 --> 01:31:05.045
apart, then all of us would
have been killed everyday.

01:31:05.045 --> 01:31:08.039
[? Matter of fact, ?] we use
the electric outlet, or our car,

01:31:08.039 --> 01:31:13.029
or our toaster, or whatever we
use to make [? our weapons. ?]

01:31:13.029 --> 01:31:16.023
More people go to school
today than ever before

01:31:16.023 --> 01:31:17.934
in technologically
advanced society.

01:31:17.934 --> 01:31:19.017
And that's a humane thing.

01:31:19.017 --> 01:31:24.007
More people live in their home
without being sick and hungry,

01:31:24.007 --> 01:31:27.001
and they do so
because of technology.

01:31:27.001 --> 01:31:29.496
So I'm not saying that
things are perfect.

01:31:29.496 --> 01:31:32.490
And there's always lots
of room for improvement.

01:31:32.490 --> 01:31:35.484
And engineers, especially
engineers from MIT,

01:31:35.484 --> 01:31:37.979
and scientists, and social
scientists, and economists,

01:31:37.979 --> 01:31:40.973
and others should always
be working to improve.

01:31:40.973 --> 01:31:44.466
But it's a serious
mistake, a serious mistake,

01:31:44.466 --> 01:31:46.961
to say that because
of technology,

01:31:46.961 --> 01:31:49.955
we have become [? idly ?]
[? humane ?] or that people

01:31:49.955 --> 01:31:53.448
that are [? ahead, ?] on
top of our industries,

01:31:53.448 --> 01:31:56.941
don't care about [INAUDIBLE].

01:31:56.941 --> 01:32:01.458
The evidence simply is not
that because if you looked

01:32:01.458 --> 01:32:12.040
at the [INAUDIBLE] [? and the ?]
industries around the world,

01:32:12.040 --> 01:32:15.290
you can find any
[? deterrences ?] from other

01:32:15.290 --> 01:32:19.028
companies [INAUDIBLE]

01:32:19.028 --> 01:32:21.518
Or [? look ?] [? at the ?]
alternatives [? when people ?]

01:32:21.518 --> 01:32:25.502
suffer in Sudan, Ethiopia,
or India, or Southeast Asia.

01:32:25.502 --> 01:32:26.996
But they don't have
any technology.

01:32:26.996 --> 01:32:29.486
They don't have any
of these industries.

01:32:29.486 --> 01:32:34.466
Then the evidence would be
overwhelming about the benefits

01:32:34.466 --> 01:32:35.960
that we derive from technology.

01:32:35.960 --> 01:32:38.905
So it's not technology
that is at fault. It's not

01:32:38.905 --> 01:32:39.540
our managers.

01:32:39.540 --> 01:32:43.682
It is that we are not perfect as
human beings like [INAUDIBLE]..

01:32:43.682 --> 01:32:47.948
We recognize the evidence that
they shouldn't get [? after ?]

01:32:47.948 --> 01:32:58.094
[INAUDIBLE] and the other guy
he was distressed [INAUDIBLE]

01:32:58.094 --> 01:33:00.559
but after a while
they [INAUDIBLE]..

01:33:00.559 --> 01:33:03.517
So the point that I want
to make is the following,

01:33:03.517 --> 01:33:06.220
I know no engineers,
whether in management,

01:33:06.220 --> 01:33:11.342
or at the lower [? echelon, ?]
who willingly and knowingly

01:33:11.342 --> 01:33:14.457
proceed with doing
something that

01:33:14.457 --> 01:33:16.832
will be destructive to society.

01:33:16.832 --> 01:33:21.930
Maybe, there is a [INAUDIBLE]
area in which [INAUDIBLE]

01:33:21.930 --> 01:33:25.770
the other person is absolutely
[? certain ?] [INAUDIBLE]

01:33:25.770 --> 01:33:30.377
and sometimes one person comes
out who's a [? judgment ?] one

01:33:30.377 --> 01:33:32.560
way, and other
times, another way,

01:33:32.560 --> 01:33:35.470
but not because they do
not care about society.

01:33:35.470 --> 01:33:39.602
As an engineer, I
refuse to accept

01:33:39.602 --> 01:33:42.770
that there are, in general,
not the exceptions,

01:33:42.770 --> 01:33:45.119
engineers who simply
don't care because they

01:33:45.119 --> 01:33:46.796
are managers [INAUDIBLE].

01:33:52.070 --> 01:33:54.430
BOISJOLY: Yeah, I
guess only one comment,

01:33:54.430 --> 01:33:58.420
and I agree with a significant
part of what you say.

01:33:58.420 --> 01:34:03.400
But in another sense, I
disagree because how can you

01:34:03.400 --> 01:34:11.680
explain the total destruction of
the work ethic in this country,

01:34:11.680 --> 01:34:15.580
if you don't lay it at the feet
of the people who are charged

01:34:15.580 --> 01:34:18.100
with running the
corporate structure that's

01:34:18.100 --> 01:34:19.930
responsible for doing this.

01:34:19.930 --> 01:34:21.820
We are no longer, as
the United States,

01:34:21.820 --> 01:34:23.830
able to compete in the
world economy because

01:34:23.830 --> 01:34:24.890
of this situation.

01:34:24.890 --> 01:34:27.260
They've focused on
short-term profits

01:34:27.260 --> 01:34:31.542
at the almost total
exclusion of everything else.

01:34:31.542 --> 01:34:34.060
And then they are
responsible for that.

01:34:34.060 --> 01:34:35.710
I've seen it in my own industry.

01:34:35.710 --> 01:34:37.720
I watched it happened.

01:34:37.720 --> 01:34:39.760
And I agree with you.

01:34:39.760 --> 01:34:44.830
To a person, nobody openly
wants somebody to get killed.

01:34:44.830 --> 01:34:48.010
Nobody openly plans
on something failing.

01:34:48.010 --> 01:34:49.390
I agree with you.

01:34:49.390 --> 01:34:53.200
But they, at the top,
never go down on the floor,

01:34:53.200 --> 01:34:54.940
and kick the tires,
and find out what's

01:34:54.940 --> 01:34:56.710
going on at the low level.

01:34:56.710 --> 01:34:59.350
And it's not all their
fault, with regard

01:34:59.350 --> 01:35:01.280
to what they make
the decisions on,

01:35:01.280 --> 01:35:06.040
because the corollary to that is
that the bad news gets filtered

01:35:06.040 --> 01:35:09.760
out so badly on the way up
that they get the impression

01:35:09.760 --> 01:35:11.290
that everything is well.

01:35:11.290 --> 01:35:13.870
And in 27 years,
I've never understood

01:35:13.870 --> 01:35:18.160
why a CEO, if he did not have
the time because of pressing

01:35:18.160 --> 01:35:21.820
needs at his level, did
not find a confidante

01:35:21.820 --> 01:35:24.100
to go down, and kick
the tires for him,

01:35:24.100 --> 01:35:26.084
and report back to him or her.

01:35:26.084 --> 01:35:27.250
And they've never done that.

01:35:27.250 --> 01:35:28.990
And I've never understood
that philosophy

01:35:28.990 --> 01:35:31.510
because their companies,
generally, are coming down

01:35:31.510 --> 01:35:32.590
around their necks.

01:35:32.590 --> 01:35:34.450
And they don't
even know about it.

01:35:34.450 --> 01:35:36.460
We are not competing
in the world economy,

01:35:36.460 --> 01:35:39.280
not because we don't have the
talent, not because we don't

01:35:39.280 --> 01:35:41.740
have the people,
or the technology,

01:35:41.740 --> 01:35:46.210
we do not have work productivity
within that framework anymore.

01:35:46.210 --> 01:35:48.280
And that's the
unfortunate consequence

01:35:48.280 --> 01:35:49.940
of what has happened.

01:35:49.940 --> 01:35:52.270
And so I just wanted to
interject that part of it.

